{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794528520028,"sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","part":{"partID":"prt_1d23c24b9001L6L6lSdBe4MKT0","sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","messageID":"msg_1d23c0a6e0019tmQfbw4yQlp70","type":"tool","id":"call_b5dae66b93ed43afa87f17c8","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/codex-hook-approval-check.cjs"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/codex-hook-approval-check.cjs, lines 1-214\n1: #!/usr/bin/env node\n2: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n3: // MODULE: Codex Hook Approval Check\n4: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n5: 'use strict';\n6: \n7: // Codex runs a project hook only after the operator approves that exact entry.\n8: // The approval lives in config.toml under a hooks.state key as a trusted_hash,\n9: // and an unapproved entry is skipped without a warning. A wired advisor hook can\n10: // therefore be present in .codex/hooks.json and still never deliver a brief,\n11: // which no diff can tell apart from a hook that was never wired.\n12: //\n13: // This check is read-only on purpose. The approval belongs to the /hooks review\n14: // in a Codex session, so the repair line names that step instead of offering to\n15: // copy a hash. The hash value stays out of every output because echoing a\n16: // credential serves no diagnostic purpose.\n17: //\n18: // Exit codes:\n19: //   0  the project has no hooks file, or the advisor entry is approved\n20: //   1  the advisor entry is absent, or present and unapproved\n21: //   2  bad argument\n22: //\n23: // Usage: codex-hook-approval-check.cjs [--root <dir>] [--config <path>] [--json]\n24: //   --root    project root whose .codex/hooks.json is read, defaults to the cwd\n25: //   --config  Codex config file, defaults to $CODEX_HOME/config.toml\n26: //   --json    print one JSON object instead of the text lines\n27: \n28: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n29: // 1. IMPORTS\n30: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n31: \n32: const fs = require('node:fs');\n33: const os = require('node:os');\n34: const path = require('node:path');\n35: \n36: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n37: // 2. CONSTANTS\n38: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n39: \n40: const ADVISOR_MARKER = 'hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js';\n41: const EVENT_KEY = 'user_prompt_submit';\n42: const USAGE = 'Usage: codex-hook-approval-check.cjs [--root <dir>] [--config <path>] [--json]\\n';\n43: \n44: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n45: // 3. HELPERS\n46: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n47: \n48: // A misspelled or value-less flag must fail loudly rather than read another root.\n49: function parseArgs(argv) {\n50:   const options = { root: process.cwd(), config: null, json: false };\n51:   for (let index = 0; index < argv.length; index += 1) {\n52:     const arg = argv[index];\n53:     if (arg === '--json') {\n54:       options.json = true;\n55:       continue;\n56:     }\n57:     if (arg === '--root' || arg === '--config') {\n58:       const value = argv[index + 1];\n59:       if (value === undefined || value === '') return null;\n60:       options[arg.slice(2)] = value;\n61:       index += 1;\n62:       continue;\n63:     }\n64:     return null;\n65:   }\n66:   return options;\n67: }\n68: \n69: function defaultConfigFile() {\n70:   const codexHome = process.env.CODEX_HOME || path.join(os.homedir(), '.codex');\n71:   return path.join(codexHome, 'config.toml');\n72: }\n73: \n74: function trimSeparators(value) {\n75:   const trimmed = value.replace(/[\\\\/]+$/, '');\n76:   return trimmed === '' ? value : trimmed;\n77: }\n78: \n79: // Codex keys an approval by the hooks file, the event, the group and the hook\n80: // index, so any edit that moves the advisor entry needs a fresh approval. The\n81: // caller may pass a relative or symlinked root while Codex recorded the real\n82: // path, so both forms are tried.\n83: function hookStateKeys(root, group, index) {\n84:   const roots = [trimSeparators(root)];\n85:   try {\n86:     roots.push(trimSeparators(fs.realpathSync(root)));\n87:   } catch {\n88:     // A root that cannot be resolved has only the form the caller gave.\n89:   }\n90:   const keys = [];\n91:   for (const base of roots) {\n92:     const key = `${base}/.codex/hooks.json:${EVENT_KEY}:${group}:${index}`;\n93:     if (!keys.includes(key)) keys.push(key);\n94:   }\n95:   return keys;\n96: }\n97: \n98: // An unparseable hooks file cannot hold a findable entry, so it takes the same\n99: // verdict as a file that lacks the entry. Either way the hook cannot run.\n100: function findAdvisorEntry(hooksFile) {\n101:   if (!fs.existsSync(hooksFile)) return { kind: 'skip' };\n102:   let document;\n103:   try {\n104:     document = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(hooksFile, 'utf8'));\n105:   } catch {\n106:     return { kind: 'absent' };\n107:   }\n108:   const groups = document?.hooks?.UserPromptSubmit;\n109:   if (!Array.isArray(groups)) return { kind: 'absent' };\n110:   for (const [groupIndex, group] of groups.entries()) {\n111:     const hooks = Array.isArray(group?.hooks) ? group.hooks : [];\n112:     for (const [hookIndex, hook] of hooks.entries()) {\n113:       if (typeof hook?.command === 'string' && hook.command.includes(ADVISOR_MARKER)) {\n114:         return { kind: 'present', group: groupIndex, index: hookIndex };\n115:       }\n116:     }\n117:   }\n118:   return { kind: 'absent' };\n119: }\n120: \n121: // The approval block ends at the next TOML header, so a trusted_hash recorded\n122: // under a different hooks.state key must not count for this entry.\n123: function approvalFound(configFile, keys) {\n124:   let text;\n125:   try {\n126:     text = fs.readFileSync(configFile, 'utf8');\n127:   } catch {\n128:     return false;\n129:   }\n130:   const lines = text.split(/\\r?\\n/);\n131:   const headers = new Set(keys.map((key) => `[hooks.state.\"${key}\"]`));\n132:   for (let index = 0; index < lines.length; index += 1) {\n133:     if (!headers.has(lines[index].trim())) continue;\n134:     for (let cursor = index + 1; cursor < lines.length; cursor += 1) {\n135:       const line = lines[cursor].trim();\n136:       if (line.startsWith('[')) break;\n137:       if (/^trusted_hash\\s*=/.test(line)) return true;\n138:     }\n139:   }\n140:   return false;\n141: }\n142: \n143: function report(options, verdict, lines) {\n144:   if (options.json) {\n145:     process.stdout.write(`${JSON.stringify(verdict, null, 2)}\\n`);\n146:     return;\n147:   }\n148:   for (const line of lines) process.stdout.write(`${line}\\n`);\n149: }\n150: \n151: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n152: // 4. CORE LOGIC\n153: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n154: \n155: function main(argv) {\n156:   const options = parseArgs(argv);\n157:   if (options === null) {\n158:     process.stderr.write(USAGE);\n159:     return 2;\n160:   }\n161: \n162:   const root = options.root;\n163:   const hooksFile = path.join(root, '.codex', 'hooks.json');\n164:   const entry = findAdvisorEntry(hooksFile);\n165: \n166:   if (entry.kind === 'skip') {\n167:     report(options, { status: 'skip', code: 'no-codex-hooks', root }, ['SKIP no-codex-hooks']);\n168:     return 0;\n169:   }\n170: \n171:   if (entry.kind === 'absent') {\n172:     report(options, {\n173:       status: 'warn',\n174:       code: 'codex-advisor-hook-absent',\n175:       root,\n176:       hooksFile,\n177:     }, ['WARN codex-advisor-hook-absent']);\n178:     return 1;\n179:   }\n180: \n181:   const configFile = options.config || defaultConfigFile();\n182:   const keys = hookStateKeys(root, entry.group, entry.index);\n183:   if (approvalFound(configFile, keys)) {\n184:     report(options, {\n185:       status: 'pass',\n186:       code: 'codex-hooks-approved',\n187:       root,\n188:       group: entry.group,\n189:       index: entry.index,\n190:       config: configFile,\n191:     }, ['PASS codex-hooks-approved']);\n192:     return 0;\n193:   }\n194: \n195:   const repair = `repair: open /hooks in a Codex session at ${root} and approve each entry`;\n196:   report(options, {\n197:     status: 'warn',\n198:     code: 'codex-hooks-unapproved',\n199:     root,\n200:     group: entry.group,\n201:     index: entry.index,\n202:     config: configFile,\n203:     repair,\n204:   }, [`WARN codex-hooks-unapproved ${root}`, repair]);\n205:   return 1;\n206: }\n207: \n208: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n209: // 5. ENTRY POINT\n210: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n211: \n212: if (require.main === module) process.exitCode = main(process.argv.slice(2));\n213: \n214: module.exports = { main, findAdvisorEntry, approvalFound };","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/codex-hook-approval-check.cjs, lines 1-214\n1: #!/usr/bin/env node\n2: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n3: // MODULE: Codex Hook Approval Check\n4: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n5: 'use strict';\n6: \n7: // Codex runs a project hook only after the operator approves that exact entry.\n8: // The approval lives in config.toml under a hooks.state key as a trusted_hash,\n9: // and an unapproved entry is skipped without a warning. A wired advisor hook can\n10: // therefore be present in .codex/hooks.json and still never deliver a brief,\n11: // which no diff can tell apart from a hook that was never wired.\n12: //\n13: // This check is read-only on purpose. The approval belongs to the /hooks review\n14: // in a Codex session, so the repair line names that step instead of offering to\n15: // copy a hash. The hash value stays out of every output because echoing a\n16: // credential serves no diagnostic purpose.\n17: //\n18: // Exit codes:\n19: //   0  the project has no hooks file, or the advisor entry is approved\n20: //   1  the advisor entry is absent, or present and unapproved\n21: //   2  bad argument\n22: //\n23: // Usage: codex-hook-approval-check.cjs [--root <dir>] [--config <path>] [--json]\n24: //   --root    project root whose .codex/hooks.json is read, defaults to the cwd\n25: //   --config  Codex config file, defaults to $CODEX_HOME/config.toml\n26: //   --json    print one JSON object instead of the text lines\n27: \n28: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n29: // 1. IMPORTS\n30: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n31: \n32: const fs = require('node:fs');\n33: const os = require('node:os');\n34: const path = require('node:path');\n35: \n36: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n37: // 2. CONSTANTS\n38: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n39: \n40: const ADVISOR_MARKER = 'hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js';\n41: const EVENT_KEY = 'user_prompt_submit';\n42: const USAGE = 'Usage: codex-hook-approval-check.cjs [--root <dir>] [--config <path>] [--json]\\n';\n43: \n44: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n45: // 3. HELPERS\n46: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n47: \n48: // A misspelled or value-less flag must fail loudly rather than read another root.\n49: function parseArgs(argv) {\n50:   const options = { root: process.cwd(), config: null, json: false };\n51:   for (let index = 0; index < argv.length; index += 1) {\n52:     const arg = argv[index];\n53:     if (arg === '--json') {\n54:       options.json = true;\n55:       continue;\n56:     }\n57:     if (arg === '--root' || arg === '--config') {\n58:       const value = argv[index + 1];\n59:       if (value === undefined || value === '') return null;\n60:       options[arg.slice(2)] = value;\n61:       index += 1;\n62:       continue;\n63:     }\n64:     return null;\n65:   }\n66:   return options;\n67: }\n68: \n69: function defaultConfigFile() {\n70:   const codexHome = process.env.CODEX_HOME || path.join(os.homedir(), '.codex');\n71:   return path.join(codexHome, 'config.toml');\n72: }\n73: \n74: function trimSeparators(value) {\n75:   const trimmed = value.replace(/[\\\\/]+$/, '');\n76:   return trimmed === '' ? value : trimmed;\n77: }\n78: \n79: // Codex keys an approval by the hooks file, the event, the group and the hook\n80: // index, so any edit that moves the advisor entry needs a fresh approval. The\n81: // caller may pass a relative or symlinked root while Codex recorded the real\n82: // path, so both forms are tried.\n83: function hookStateKeys(root, group, index) {\n84:   const roots = [trimSeparators(root)];\n85:   try {\n86:     roots.push(trimSeparators(fs.realpathSync(root)));\n87:   } catch {\n88:     // A root that cannot be resolved has only the form the caller gave.\n89:   }\n90:   const keys = [];\n91:   for (const base of roots) {\n92:     const key = `${base}/.codex/hooks.json:${EVENT_KEY}:${group}:${index}`;\n93:     if (!keys.includes(key)) keys.push(key);\n94:   }\n95:   return keys;\n96: }\n97: \n98: // An unparseable hooks file cannot hold a findable entry, so it takes the same\n99: // verdict as a file that lacks the entry. Either way the hook cannot run.\n100: function findAdvisorEntry(hooksFile) {\n101:   if (!fs.existsSync(hooksFile)) return { kind: 'skip' };\n102:   let document;\n103:   try {\n104:     document = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(hooksFile, 'utf8'));\n105:   } catch {\n106:     return { kind: 'absent' };\n107:   }\n108:   const groups = document?.hooks?.UserPromptSubmit;\n109:   if (!Array.isArray(groups)) return { kind: 'absent' };\n110:   for (const [groupIndex, group] of groups.entries()) {\n111:     const hooks = Array.isArray(group?.hooks) ? group.hooks : [];\n112:     for (const [hookIndex, hook] of hooks.entries()) {\n113:       if (typeof hook?.command === 'string' && hook.command.includes(ADVISOR_MARKER)) {\n114:         return { kind: 'present', group: groupIndex, index: hookIndex };\n115:       }\n116:     }\n117:   }\n118:   return { kind: 'absent' };\n119: }\n120: \n121: // The approval block ends at the next TOML header, so a trusted_hash recorded\n122: // under a different hooks.state key must not count for this entry.\n123: function approvalFound(configFile, keys) {\n124:   let text;\n125:   try {\n126:     text = fs.readFileSync(configFile, 'utf8');\n127:   } catch {\n128:     return false;\n129:   }\n130:   const lines = text.split(/\\r?\\n/);\n131:   const headers = new Set(keys.map((key) => `[hooks.state.\"${key}\"]`));\n132:   for (let index = 0; index < lines.length; index += 1) {\n133:     if (!headers.has(lines[index].trim())) continue;\n134:     for (let cursor = index + 1; cursor < lines.length; cursor += 1) {\n135:       const line = lines[cursor].trim();\n136:       if (line.startsWith('[')) break;\n137:       if (/^trusted_hash\\s*=/.test(line)) return true;\n138:     }\n139:   }\n140:   return false;\n141: }\n142: \n143: function report(options, verdict, lines) {\n144:   if (options.json) {\n145:     process.stdout.write(`${JSON.stringify(verdict, null, 2)}\\n`);\n146:     return;\n147:   }\n148:   for (const line of lines) process.stdout.write(`${line}\\n`);\n149: }\n150: \n151: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n152: // 4. CORE LOGIC\n153: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n154: \n155: function main(argv) {\n156:   const options = parseArgs(argv);\n157:   if (options === null) {\n158:     process.stderr.write(USAGE);\n159:     return 2;\n160:   }\n161: \n162:   const root = options.root;\n163:   const hooksFile = path.join(root, '.codex', 'hooks.json');\n164:   const entry = findAdvisorEntry(hooksFile);\n165: \n166:   if (entry.kind === 'skip') {\n167:     report(options, { status: 'skip', code: 'no-codex-hooks', root }, ['SKIP no-codex-hooks']);\n168:     return 0;\n169:   }\n170: \n171:   if (entry.kind === 'absent') {\n172:     report(options, {\n173:       status: 'warn',\n174:       code: 'codex-advisor-hook-absent',\n175:       root,\n176:       hooksFile,\n177:     }, ['WARN codex-advisor-hook-absent']);\n178:     return 1;\n179:   }\n180: \n181:   const configFile = options.config || defaultConfigFile();\n182:   const keys = hookStateKeys(root, entry.group, entry.index);\n183:   if (approvalFound(configFile, keys)) {\n184:     report(options, {\n185:       status: 'pass',\n186:       code: 'codex-hooks-approved',\n187:       root,\n188:       group: entry.group,\n189:       index: entry.index,\n190:       config: configFile,\n191:     }, ['PASS codex-hooks-approved']);\n192:     return 0;\n193:   }\n194: \n195:   const repair = `repair: open /hooks in a Codex session at ${root} and approve each entry`;\n196:   report(options, {\n197:     status: 'warn',\n198:     code: 'codex-hooks-unapproved',\n199:     root,\n200:     group: entry.group,\n201:     index: entry.index,\n202:     config: configFile,\n203:     repair,\n204:   }, [`WARN codex-hooks-unapproved ${root}`, repair]);\n205:   return 1;\n206: }\n207: \n208: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n209: // 5. ENTRY POINT\n210: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n211: \n212: if (require.main === module) process.exitCode = main(process.argv.slice(2));\n213: \n214: module.exports = { main, findAdvisorEntry, approvalFound };"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/codex-hook-approval-check.cjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794528519353,"end":1794528520028}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794528520032,"sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","part":{"partID":"prt_1d23c22f9002qng5n7iqIF6dgo","sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","messageID":"msg_1d23c0a6e0019tmQfbw4yQlp70","type":"tool","id":"call_63a5fc17525b4dd2ac7c801e","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown, lines 1-266\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Doctor Command Scripts\"\n3: description: \"Developer reference for doctor routing, MCP diagnostics, release updates and structural integrity checks.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"doctor command scripts\"\n6:   - \"doctor route validation\"\n7:   - \"mcp diagnostics\"\n8: importance_tier: \"important\"\n9: ---\n10: \n11: # Doctor Command Scripts\n12: \n13: > Documents `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/`. This README uses the `.markdown` extension because\n14: > OpenCode registers every `.md` file under `.skilled/commands/` as a command.\n15: >\n16: > Diagnostic and validation entrypoints used by the doctor command family.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW AND PURPOSE\n21: \n22: `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/` contains shell, JavaScript and Python tools that support doctor routes.\n23: \n24: The scripts validate route manifests, inspect MCP installations, audit parent skill hubs, report graph freshness, check a skill's distance from the Skilled format and convert one, read the root instruction file against the shipped template and embed or restore it, then plan and apply framework release updates. Most are read-only. The release updater's apply and rollback paths write local state, and so does the skill converter's `--apply`. Every script has a test under `tests/`, except `skill-graph-freshness.cjs`, whose freshness panel test lives in the skill advisor runtime. One runner executes them all.\n25: \n26: ---\n27: \n28: ## 2. DIRECTORY TREE\n29: \n30: ```text\n31: .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/\n32: +-- agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs\n33: +-- agents-md-check.cjs\n34: +-- agents-md-embed.cjs\n35: +-- agents-md-manifest.cjs\n36: +-- audit_descriptions.py\n37: +-- command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs\n38: +-- check-mcp-mutation-class.sh\n39: +-- ci-report.cjs\n40: +-- ci-scaffold.cjs\n41: +-- ci-toggle.cjs\n42: +-- doctor-helper-match.cjs\n43: +-- doctor-phrase-reach.cjs\n44: +-- git-hook-gates.cjs\n45: +-- git-setup-check.cjs\n46: +-- git-standards.cjs\n47: +-- mcp-doctor-lib.sh\n48: +-- mcp-doctor.sh\n49: +-- opencode-version-battery.cjs\n50: +-- orphan-process-check.cjs\n51: +-- parent-skill-check.cjs\n52: +-- route-validate.py\n53: +-- route-validate.sh\n54: +-- release-update.cjs\n55: +-- sk-code-quality-config.cjs\n56: +-- sk-code-setup-check.cjs\n57: +-- sk-skill-check.cjs\n58: +-- sk-skill-convert.cjs\n59: +-- skill-graph-freshness.cjs\n60: +-- lib/\n61: |   +-- agents-md-sections.cjs      # the one segmenter, classifier, block reader and delivery measurement the two engines and their suites share\n62: |   +-- ci-profile.cjs             # the repository CI profile loader the report, scaffold and toggle scripts share\n63: |   +-- ci-report-render.cjs       # the report document, its JSON form and its fixed-width table\n64: |   +-- ci-report-sources.cjs      # repository variables, the branch runs come from and the newest non-rebuild commit\n65: |   +-- ci-report-text.cjs         # the one-line rule a gh failure and a YAML parse error both need\n66: |   +-- ci-report-workflows.cjs    # workflow discovery, the YAML parse, switch states and drift flags\n67: |   +-- quality-applied-rules.cjs  # the checklists and override rows the gate would apply, asked through --explain\n68: |   +-- quality-config-init.cjs    # the one mode that writes, from the shipped template with --apply\n69: |   +-- quality-config-source.cjs  # the config source, its trust state and the hygiene rules it switches off\n70: |   +-- quality-report-view.cjs    # the read-only status and check modes\n71: |   +-- quality-script-io.cjs      # the request error and the one place a report reaches stdout\n72: |   +-- quality-skill-paths.cjs    # the one sk-code quality folder and the handles every mode reads\n73: |   +-- sk-skill-convert-transforms.cjs # the pure frontmatter, router-move and legacy-router text transforms\n74: |   `-- sk-skill-surface.cjs       # shared probes the check engine and the converter both call: locate, classify, collisions, spawn\n75: +-- tests/            # one suite per script, plus run-all.sh\n76: |   `-- opencode-version-battery.test.cjs\n77: `-- README.markdown\n78: ```\n79: \n80: ---\n81: \n82: ## 3. KEY FILES\n83: \n84: | File | Responsibility |\n85: |---|---|\n86: | `route-validate.sh` | Runs route-manifest validation and fixture-based self-tests. |\n87: | `route-validate.py` | Implements route schema, asset, tool, mutation and display-parity checks. |\n88: | `mcp-doctor.sh` | Diagnoses supported MCP servers and runtime configuration wiring. |\n89: | `mcp-doctor-lib.sh` | Supplies logging, result tracking, JSON output and configuration helpers to `mcp-doctor.sh`. |\n90: | `check-mcp-mutation-class.sh` | Enforces read-only and mutating classifications for MCP doctor and installer scripts. |\n91: | `parent-skill-check.cjs` | Audits parent skill hubs against structural and routing invariants. |\n92: | `release-update.cjs` | Plans, aligns, applies, rolls back and records framework release bases for the five `/doctor:update` workflows. |\n93: | `skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | Compares compiled, SQLite and on-disk skill graph representations without writing. |\n94: | `sk-skill-check.cjs` | The `/doctor:sk-skill check` engine. With no argument it prints one row per direct child of `.skilled/skills`, and with a skill name or a path one line per row `C1` to `C13`, each carrying its own status and detail. A skill inside another repository's `.skilled/skills` is read against that repository, and each of its rows names the repository it read. A tool that repository does not ship runs from the checker's own copy, and the row names that copy too. It calls the format gate, the root-metadata gate, the derived-block regeneration in dry-run form, the router-equivalence replay, the advisor graph and mirror checks and the description audit, and writes nothing. `--into-hub <hub>` prints the eleven surfaces a skill or mode must land on under that hub instead, each with the probe it ran, and still writes nothing. `--json` prints either report and nothing else. It exits 0 when no row is a finding, 1 when one is, and 2 when a precondition it needs is missing, with the provision command on its own line. |\n95: | `sk-skill-convert.cjs` | Plans, and on request applies, the conversion of one skill. `plan` reads, refuses and prints every step, `apply` prints the same plan and writes only with `--apply`, and `preserve --snapshot <dir>` proves a reshaped body kept every original line. An older Skilled skill converts in place, with its machine router moved verbatim into a new sibling `ROUTER.md`. A plain skill reached through a path outside the skills root is staged under its frontmatter name and becomes a class S standalone root, and it converts only once the operator approves at least eight intent phrases, which become its `intent_signals`. A skill of neither shape is out of scope. Preflight refuses a hub declaration, a nested packet tree, a `local-` or `agent-` prefixed name, a name collision and a description over 130 characters. A skill that declares no `allowed-tools` field prints `allowed-tools: approval required; propose [...]` in its plan, and the apply writes no grant until a list is passed. A field declared twice is refused as `ALLOWED_TOOLS_DUPLICATE` rather than merged. The plain path keeps its agent output, its preservation snapshot and its staging copy in one scratch root beside the backup at `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/scratch/`, deleted when a question is declined and once the run has reported. A skill whose router already lives in `ROUTER.md` gets its frontmatter normalized and nothing else. Every write is preceded by a backup under `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/`, `ROUTER.md` is created exclusively, and a parsed router that would change restores the backup. After both format gates it runs the integration rows: the advisor scan, the compiled skill graph, the reach probe with the budget row, the regression cases and the changelog file and link. |\n96: | `sk-code-setup-check.cjs` | Runs the sk-code setup battery from a repository root: one verdict per setup leg, one repair command per failing leg and no writes. Its quality leg runs the quality runner's own tests, which build throwaway repositories under the OS temp folder and remove them. |\n97: | `sk-code-quality-config.cjs` | The quality config doctor's entry, kept thin: the arguments, the run context, the one call that starts the runner, the mode table and the exit status are here, and each mode sits in the quality modules under `lib/`. It reports which quality config the sk-code quality gate reads, whether each tool it names is on PATH, and the trust state the runner applies to that config: `trusted`, `untrusted` or `none`. `status` and `check` both print it, and when the config is untrusted they print `git config --local skilled.trustRepoChecks true` as the repair. It prints the hygiene layers, a `Hygiene:` line naming the core rules `commentHygiene.disableRules` switches off and an applied-rules block built by calling `quality-gate.cjs --explain` over a sample of one file per first three folders and extension: each checklist with its seat, its override rows or the reasons its section was refused, each same-level conflict and a `Hygiene rules:` line for the rules in force. A refused section, an unknown rule id or a missing checklist fails the status with `CONFIG_INVALID` or `CHECKLIST_MISSING`, and a conflict is listed without failing it. It validates that config and creates `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` once from the shipped template for `/doctor:sk-code quality`, and in a repository with no `specs/` folder its plan suggests the spec-kit hygiene rules without writing them. It never runs a command the config names. It exits 0 on success, 1 on an invalid or missing config, a refused checklist section or a refused init and 2 on a usage error or outside a git repository. |\n98: | `audit_descriptions.py` | Audits description lengths across skills, commands and agents. |\n99: | `agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs` | Verifies every canonical agent reaches all five runtime surfaces, and that mirror surfaces stay symlinked rather than forked. `--check` is accepted and ignored, because it is read-only either way. |\n100: | `agents-md-check.cjs` | The read-only AGENTS.md check: twelve rows reporting the install state, the sections lost or reordered, the currency of each body, every pin, the delivery budget, the shape of the repository block, the repository's own content and its local edits, each runtime copy, the `REPO RULES.md` router and the source template's purity. Three of them run their owner as a child instead of re-deriving it, carrying the rule-copy canary's budget lines, the gate 1 pointer sync's Cursor block and the repo-rules checker's summary. `--root <dir>` classifies another repository and `--json` prints the same report for a machine. A row it cannot prove reads `UNKNOWN` rather than `PASS`, so it exits 0 on no finding, 1 on a finding or an unproven row and 2 on a failed precondition. |\n101: | `agents-md-embed.cjs` | The writing half of `/doctor:agents-md`, in three subcommands. `plan` composes every target in memory and prints the targets, the diffs, the new block, the byte budget, the preservation ledger, the backup folder and the plan digest, and writes nothing. `apply --plan-digest <digest>` recomposes, refuses a digest the tree no longer produces, backs up every path it will touch, writes only inside the allowlist it checks in code and then proves the composition against the bytes on disk, restoring from the backup and ending `STATUS=FAIL` when one line went missing. `restore --run=<dir>` puts back exactly the paths that run's `manifest.json` records, removes the files, and the empty folders, that run created, and keeps the backup. It exits 0 when there was nothing to do or the work finished, 1 on a failed proof or a declined run and 2 on a failed precondition or a path outside the allowlist. |\n102: | `agents-md-manifest.cjs` | Generates and gates the shipped manifest's `derived` key: a copy of every shipped body, its hash and the router rows, taken from the root documents. `--write` refreshes it and `--check` is the freshness gate, so a body that drifted from the template fails here instead of surfacing as a check finding. It refuses a heading no authored entry matches, an authored entry that matches nothing, and text above the first heading, and `--write` runs only in the repository that ships the template. Each of the three engines has its own suite: `agents-md-check.test.cjs`, `agents-md-embed.test.cjs` and `agents-md-manifest.test.cjs`, driven from the recipe folders under `tests/fixtures/agents-md/`. |\n103: | `git-hook-gates.cjs` | Lists the shipped hook gates from `scripts/git-hooks/lib/gates.tsv` with their saved `speckit.hooks.<key>` values, and sets or unsets one in local or global git config for `/doctor:git hooks`. Refuses the per-push approvals. |\n104: | `git-standards.cjs` | Shows where a repository's commit, PR and branch rules come from, copies the shipped sk-git templates into `.sk-git/`, and changes a rules block setting or removes a kind's rules section in those copies for `/doctor:git standards`, re-validating each change with sk-git's own contract check. |\n105: | `git-setup-check.cjs` | Runs the read-only sk-git setup battery for `/doctor:git setup`: eleven checks, each PASS, FAIL, SKIP, UNKNOWN or DEGRADED with the source it read and one repair per failure. It exits 0 when every check passes or skips, 3 when none failed but a check is UNKNOWN or DEGRADED, 1 on any failure and 2 on a usage error. It loads every helper from its own install root and never runs code from the repository it inspects. |\n106: | `command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs` | Compares every command catalog and hub metadata entry back to the command's own frontmatter, which is the one copy nothing else derives from. Structural drift fails. Prose divergence reports and fails only under `--strict`. `--check` is accepted and ignored, because it is read-only either way. |\n107: | `doctor-helper-match.cjs` | Ranks the doctor commands of `_routes.yaml` against a problem stated in plain words for `/doctor:helper`: one entry per route, standalone command, standalone action and mcp sub-action, scored by inverse-document-frequency overlap over the manifest phrases plus a bonus for a fully covered phrase, with a preference for the mutation class the operator asked for. Each candidate carries the manifest phrase that best matched and the terms it matched, so the reason on every line is evidence. It skips its own route and every private name, reads one YAML file and writes nothing. It exits 0 on a match, 1 on no candidate above the floor and 2 on a bad argument, an unreadable manifest or a missing YAML parser. |\n108: | `doctor-phrase-reach.cjs` | Scores every activated doctor phrase and each runnable doctor command through the built advisor scorer, then keeps the three results the hooks show. It fails when a phrase reaches neither the top pick nor the second line. It also fails if a runnable command does not rank first or an activated phrase has fewer than three words. It exits 2 when the advisor build is missing or stale. |\n109: | `opencode-version-battery.cjs` | Checks this repository's OpenCode plugins on OpenCode 1 and 2. On 1 it proves plugin discovery through `debug config` and plugin load through an import check. On 2 it starts and stops a private server with the v2 binary it selected (`$OPENCODE_BIN`, `opencode` or `opencode2`, whichever reports a v2 version) and reads plugin and skill state from it. It exits 0 when every requested leg passes, 1 on a failed leg, 2 on a usage error, a crash or an `--out` file it cannot write and 3 when a leg is degraded or skipped. |\n110: | `ci-report.cjs` | The `/doctor:ci report` entry, kept thin: the flags, the exit codes and every name `/doctor:ci toggle` reads live here, and the readings live in the `ci-report` modules under `lib/`. It reads every workflow under `.github/workflows/` and reports its switch state, triggers, path filters and drift flags for `/doctor:ci report`. A switch is any variable a job's `if:` compares against `off`, so the workflow file is its only copy, and a repository variable value is reduced to a state and never printed. The branch, the required switch, the exempt files, the README and the rebuild trailer come from the CI profile, and the JSON names the profile it read. It exits 0 on a printed report, 1 when a workflow fails to parse, 2 on a bad argument and 3 when the CI profile is missing or invalid. |\n111: | `ci-scaffold.cjs` | Renders the sk-code-ci workflow or job template from the CI profile for `/doctor:ci scaffold`, and with `--protected` prints the profile's protected workflow names. It writes nothing. |\n112: | `ci-toggle.cjs` | Plans one switch change for `/doctor:ci toggle`: the allowlist, the refusals from the live ruleset, `neverOff` and the README, the no-op check, the exact `gh variable` command and the second named confirmation for a protected workflow. It never changes a variable. |\n113: | `lib/ci-profile.cjs` | Finds, validates and normalizes the repository CI profile (`--profile`, then `git config skcode.ciProfile`, then `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/repository/ci.json`, then a generic default that forces nothing), rejecting an unknown key. A profile whose `repository` field names another repository is ignored, and one left at `.sk-code/ci.json` is refused. |\n114: | `lib/ci-report-sources.cjs` | Reads what the report takes from outside the workflow files: the repository variables through `gh variable list`, the branch the repository integrates on and the newest commit on that branch that is not a trigger-index rebuild. Arguments go in as an array and never through a shell, a variable value is reduced to a state and never printed, and a read that fails is reported as unknown with its reason. |\n115: | `lib/ci-report-workflows.cjs` | Reads the workflow files themselves: discovery under `.github/workflows/`, the YAML parse, the switch state per job, the drift flags, the purpose column read from the workflows README and the switch names the report lists. A switch is recognized by its shape, so any repository's switch names are read and none is assumed. |\n116: | `lib/ci-report-render.cjs` | Builds the report document and its two renderings, the JSON a machine reads and the fixed-width table a person reads, both from the same readings. The not-checked list travels with the document, so a clean row is never read as a pass for a rule no parser checks. |\n117: | `lib/ci-report-text.cjs` | `firstLine()`, the one-line rule a `gh` failure and a YAML parse error both need, because a whole message would break the table row, the JSON field and the stderr line that carry it. |\n118: | `lib/quality-skill-paths.cjs` | The one sk-code quality folder this doctor reads, and the handles inside it that answer every mode's question. Each path resolves from the file's own location, so the doctor works from a checkout anywhere, and the runner, the config reader and the helpers load here so every mode reads one skill folder, one config schema and the one runner the gate itself starts. |\n119: | `lib/quality-script-io.cjs` | The two ways the quality doctor answers whoever started it: the typed request failure that carries the exit code and the `STATUS=` error code a route reads, and `emit()`, the single place a report reaches stdout. One function writes the last line, so a route reads one whatever the mode. |\n120: | `lib/quality-config-source.cjs` | Which repository config a mode read, whether the runner trusts it and which core hygiene rules it switches off, every answer read through the module the gate itself reads so a doctor verdict and a gate verdict cannot drift. It also builds the suggestion an init prints for a repository with no `specs/` folder. |\n121: | `lib/quality-config-init.cjs` | The one mode that can write: it shows the config it would create from the shipped template and writes it only with `--apply`, through a hard link from a temporary file, so it never replaces a config that already exists. An existing config is refused rather than merged. |\n122: | `lib/quality-report-view.cjs` | The two read-only modes. `status` reports the config, its trust, the tools it names, the hygiene layers and what the gate would apply here, and `check` stops at the config itself. Both render the lines a route prints and the document `--json` carries from one set of readings, and neither starts a configured check. |\n123: | `lib/quality-applied-rules.cjs` | Which checklists and hygiene rules the gate would apply to a repository and which override rows inside them it would honour, asked of the runner itself through `--explain` over a sampled file per folder group and extension, so the doctor keeps no second copy of checklist discovery or of the override grammar. |\n124: | `lib/sk-skill-surface.cjs` | The probes `/doctor:sk-skill` shares between its check engine and its converter: resolve a name or a path to a skill root, classify where that root sits (a direct child of `.skilled/skills`, outside it or nested in a hub), read the frontmatter fields, inspect name collisions against the reserved and persona prefixes, and wrap a spawned validator so its JSON, its exit status and its missing-tool cases read the same in both scripts. |\n125: | `lib/sk-skill-convert-transforms.cjs` | The pure text transforms the converter applies, kept free of the repository so they are unit-tested on strings: normalize the `version` and `allowed-tools` frontmatter, find the machine blocks a Smart Routing section still holds, move a legacy trailing machine router section into the sibling router template, and add the router link line. |\n126: | `orphan-process-check.cjs` | Lists the helper processes and model servers this user started that were reparented to init and are older than a minimum age (60 minutes by default), classifying each as a node test, vitest, tail monitor, grep search, executor worker, session-relaunch test helper, model server or git-live-follow, and reports each one's age and memory with a total. `--reap` sends `SIGTERM` after re-checking each process's identity, `--yes` confirms it, and `--json` and `--summary` print machine-readable and one-line forms. It exits 0 on a printed report, 1 on an unexpected error and 2 on a bad argument. |\n127: \n128: ---\n129: \n130: ## 4. ROUTE VALIDATION\n131: \n132: `route-validate.sh` delegates the live manifest checks to `route-validate.py`.\n133: \n134: The validator checks:\n135: \n136: - Manifest parsing and schema version\n137: - Required route fields\n138: - Unique targets\n139: - Referenced YAML assets\n140: - Mutation classes\n141: - MCP tool grants\n142: - Trigger phrases\n143: - Script path resolution\n144: - Each route names an existing doctor router\n145: - Target parity between each command's router and presentation\n146: - Read-only route mutation policy\n147: - Workflow activity coverage for every route script invocation\n148: \n149: Run it from the repository root:\n150: \n151: ```bash\n152: bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/route-validate.sh\n153: ```\n154: \n155: Run its negative fixture suite:\n156: \n157: ```bash\n158: bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/route-validate.sh --self-test\n159: ```\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 5. DIAGNOSTIC ENTRYPOINTS\n164: \n165: | Command | Purpose |\n166: |---|---|\n167: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-check.cjs [--root <dir>] [--json]` | Report the twelve AGENTS.md rows for this repository or another one, and write nothing. |\n168: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-embed.cjs plan [--root <dir>] [--keep-claude-md] [--create-router]` | Print every target with its diff, the new block, the budget, the preservation ledger, the backup folder and the plan digest. Writes nothing, and prints `NOTHING TO DO` when the repository is already in its target state. |\n169: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-embed.cjs apply --plan-digest <digest> [--root <dir>]` | Back up, write inside the allowlist and prove the composition, only with the digest the plan printed. |\n170: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-embed.cjs restore --run=<dir> --yes` | Put back exactly the paths one run recorded, and remove the files, and the empty folders, that run created. |\n171: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-manifest.cjs --check` | Fail when the manifest's derived key no longer matches the shipped documents. |\n172: | `bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/mcp-doctor.sh` | Diagnose all supported MCP servers. |\n173: | `bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/mcp-doctor.sh --json` | Emit machine-readable MCP diagnostics. |\n174: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/parent-skill-check.cjs <skill-dir>` | Audit one parent skill hub. |\n175: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | Report skill graph drift. |\n176: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-check.cjs check [<name-or-path>] [--json] [--into-hub <hub>]` | Report how far one skill, or the whole fleet, is from the Skilled format row by row, or report a skill's gaps against a hub's eleven surfaces. |\n177: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-convert.cjs plan <name-or-path>` | Print the conversion plan for one skill and write nothing. A plain skill refuses until at least eight `--approved-phrase` values are passed, and a skill with no `allowed-tools` field prints the list its plan proposes. |\n178: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-convert.cjs apply <name-or-path> --apply` | Write a plan already shown and approved: normalize the frontmatter and move a legacy machine router into `ROUTER.md`, or stage a plain skill as a new root. Without `--apply` it prints the plan and stops. `preserve --snapshot <dir>` proves the reshaped body first and writes nothing. |\n179: | `python3 .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/audit_descriptions.py --repo-root .` | Audit description budgets. |\n180: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs` | Report agent-roster coverage drift across runtimes. |\n181: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs` | Report command-catalog and hub-metadata drift. |\n182: | `bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/check-mcp-mutation-class.sh` | Check the read-only and mutating classes of MCP doctor and installer scripts. |\n183: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/release-update.cjs check --offline` | Report what a release update would change, without fetching. |\n184: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/git-hook-gates.cjs list` | Show every hook gate and its saved setting. |\n185: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/git-standards.cjs status` | Show which commit, PR and branch rules are enforced, and from where. |\n186: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/git-setup-check.cjs` | Check which sk-git hooks, rules and settings are live in this repository. |\n187: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-code-quality-config.cjs status` | Show which quality config the gate reads, whether each tool it names is on PATH, the trust state the runner applies to that config, the hygiene rules it switches off and the checklists and override rows the gate would apply. |\n188: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs` | Check this repository's OpenCode plugins on OpenCode 1 and 2. |\n189: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs --family=v1\\|v2\\|both` | Choose the family to check. The default is `both`. |\n190: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs --json` | Print the JSON summary on stdout instead of the human lines. |\n191: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs --root <dir> --out <file>` | Inspect another repository root and write the JSON summary to the named file. |\n192: \n193: The battery runs five legs:\n194: \n195: - `family-present`: PASS when a candidate's `--version` succeeds and parses to that family.\n196: - `plugin-discovered`: PASS when OpenCode 1's `debug config` exits 0 with parseable JSON, the plugin folder holds at least one top-level `.js` file, none is a broken symlink and every one is listed.\n197: - `plugin-load`: PASS when the import check exits 0 and reports every top-level `.js` file loaded.\n198: - `plugin-active`: PASS when two consecutive reads give the same non-zero count and, on that read, every top-level `.js` file has an entry under the inspected root with status active.\n199: - `skill-visible`: PASS when the skill listing parses and has an entry that mentions the inspected root.\n200: - `command-listed`: PASS when every `.md` file under the project's `.opencode/commands/` folder (or its singular `command/` form) is listed under the name it registers, which is its relative path without `.md`, or on OpenCode 1 the `name:` in its leading frontmatter when it has one, and every listed name comes from a folder the runtime reads (the global config folders, and on OpenCode 2 every ancestor `.opencode` folder plus the built-ins `init` and `review`). On OpenCode 2 a `<server>:<prompt>` name also passes when `<server>` is an MCP server in the config sources the private server reports, and a PASS notes `mcp prompts: <n>`. When the config sources did not parse, a `<server>:<prompt>` name in no command folder degrades the leg instead of reading as a stray, because the server list cannot be judged. A listed name that a non-command file in the project's command folder could produce also fails, even when a global or inherited folder holds a command of that name: any file not ending in `.md`, such as `README.markdown`, `.txt` or `.yaml`, counts under its relative path both with and without its extension. The two OpenCode 2 built-ins `init` and `review` and a `<server>:<prompt>` name from a configured MCP server are exempt, because the rule catches an OpenCode version that starts scanning more than `*.md`. A name that a `.md` command in the same folder already owns is left out, because the command answers to that name.\n201: \n202: A family that is not installed is SKIP, never FAIL.\n203: \n204: On this repository all legs pass on OpenCode 1.19.3 and 2.0.32. OpenCode 1.19.3 cuts piped stdout at 65,536 bytes, which is why the collectors read stdout from files.\n205: \n206: ---\n207: \n208: ## 6. MUTATION BOUNDARIES\n209: \n210: | Script | Boundary |\n211: |---|---|\n212: | `route-validate.sh` | Read-only during normal validation. Its self-test creates temporary fixture files and removes them on exit. |\n213: | `mcp-doctor.sh` | Read-only diagnostic. Repairs belong to the `/doctor:mcp` workflows, one approval at a time. Exit 3 means bad arguments. |\n214: | `check-mcp-mutation-class.sh` | Read-only contract scan. |\n215: | `parent-skill-check.cjs` | Read-only audit. |\n216: | `skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | Read-only report. |\n217: | `sk-skill-check.cjs` | Read-only, including the hub gap report: every validator and every hub checker runs in its check-only form and its derived-block probe is a dry run. |\n218: | `sk-skill-convert.cjs` | `plan` and `preserve` are read-only. `apply` is a dry run until `--apply`. An in-place conversion rewrites the skill's `SKILL.md` and creates its sibling `ROUTER.md`, and a plain skill is staged as a new root under `.skilled/skills/<name>/`. Both back the pre-write bytes up under `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/` and never replace a file that exists. The plain path keeps its three temporary folders in one scratch root beside them at `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/scratch/`, and deletes it when a question is declined and again once a finished run has reported. The integration rows then write the advisor graph, the compiled `skill-graph.json` and the regression cases file, and every one of them is listed in the undo block the run prints. |\n219: | `audit_descriptions.py` | Read-only audit. |\n220: | `agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs`, `command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs` | Read-only reports. |\n221: | `release-update.cjs` | `check` and `align --dry-run` are read-only. `align` writes a run directory. `apply`, `rollback` and `record-base` write framework files and release records under a lock. The `unlock` subcommand removes a lock only when its owner process is gone. |\n222: \n223: | `git-hook-gates.cjs` | `list` is read-only. `set` is a dry run until `--apply`, which writes only `speckit.hooks.<key>` in local or global git config. |\n224: | `git-standards.cjs` | `status` and `check` are read-only. `init`, `set` and `disable` are dry runs until `--apply`, which writes only `.sk-git/` or the `skgit.contractDir` directory, never the shipped sk-git templates. |\n225: | `git-setup-check.cjs` | Read-only. It writes no file and no git config value, and calls no allocator or `--apply` mode. |\n226: | `sk-code-quality-config.cjs` | `status` and `check` are read-only; `status` starts the runner's `--explain` once per batch of up to 500 sampled paths and no configured check. `init` is a dry run until `--apply`, which creates only `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` and refuses with `CONFIG_EXISTS` when that file exists. |\n227: | `agents-md-check.cjs` | Read-only over any root: it reads the instruction file, its runtime copies and `REPO RULES.md`, runs three owner tools as children in their check-only forms and writes nothing. |\n228: | `agents-md-embed.cjs` | `plan` is read-only. `apply` writes only the paths in its own allowlist, all of them under the target root, after backing each one up under `.skilled/output/doctor-agents-md/<digest>/` (or the OS temp folder when that path is not ignored), and restores from that backup itself when its post-write proof fails. `restore` writes only the paths the run's `manifest.json` records and keeps the backup folder. It never runs `git add`, `git commit` or `git stash`. |\n229: | `agents-md-manifest.cjs` | `--check` is read-only. `--write` rewrites the manifest's `derived` key and refuses to run outside the repository that ships the template. |\n230: | `opencode-version-battery.cjs` | The battery itself writes only the `--out` file and temporary folders it removes when each step ends. The private server's folder is also removed on `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM`, and the server is stopped. The import check runs each plugin's v1 factory in a child process rooted at the repository, so anything a plugin does when it loads happens during the check. It never touches the shared v2 service and never prints the private server's password. OpenCode 1 writes into a temporary data folder, not the user's. |\n231: \n232: Do not invoke a mutating path from a route classified as read-only.\n233: \n234: ---\n235: \n236: ## 7. VALIDATION AND INSTRUCTIONS\n237: \n238: Run every doctor test from the repository root. CI runs the same command in the `doctor-scripts` job of `.github/workflows/spec-kit-check.yml`:\n239: \n240: ```bash\n241: bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/tests/run-all.sh\n242: ```\n243: \n244: The runner needs `node`, `python3` with PyYAML, and the advisor runtime's dependencies (`npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime ci`). It exits `0` when every suite passes, `1` when one fails and `2` when a required tool is missing. `tests/README.markdown` lists the suites.\n245: \n246: Each script can be pointed at a fixture tree, which is how the tests run without touching the repository:\n247: \n248: | Script | Override |\n249: |---|---|\n250: | `agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs`, `command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs`, `mcp-doctor.sh`, `release-update.cjs` | `--root <dir>` |\n251: | `agents-md-check.cjs` | `--root <dir>`. It also takes `--manifest <path>` and `--source` |\n252: | `git-hook-gates.cjs`, `git-standards.cjs`, `git-setup-check.cjs`, `sk-code-quality-config.cjs` | `--repo <dir>`. `git-hook-gates.cjs` also reads `GATE_REGISTRY`, and `git-setup-check.cjs` also takes `--tool <name>` |\n253: | `audit_descriptions.py`, `route-validate.sh` | `--repo-root <dir>`. `route-validate.sh` also reads `REPO_ROOT`, `ROUTES_FILE`, `DOCTOR_DIR` and `ASSETS_DIR` |\n254: | `check-mcp-mutation-class.sh` | the repository root as its first argument |\n255: | `parent-skill-check.cjs` | `PARENT_HUB_CHECK_COMMANDS_DIR` for the commands tree. `PARENT_HUB_CHECK_STRICT=0` reports advisory findings as warnings, and hard invariants still fail |\n256: | `skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | `SKILL_GRAPH_FRESHNESS_ROOT`, `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DB_DIR` |\n257: \n258: ---\n259: \n260: ## 8. RELATED\n261: \n262: - [Doctor route manifest](../../../commands/doctor/_routes.yaml)\n263: - [Doctor command routers](../../../commands/doctor/)\n264: - [Doctor assets](../../../commands/doctor/assets/)\n265: - [Doctor script tests](./tests/README.markdown)\n266: - [Commands directory](../../../commands/)","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown, lines 1-266\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Doctor Command Scripts\"\n3: description: \"Developer reference for doctor routing, MCP diagnostics, release updates and structural integrity checks.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"doctor command scripts\"\n6:   - \"doctor route validation\"\n7:   - \"mcp diagnostics\"\n8: importance_tier: \"important\"\n9: ---\n10: \n11: # Doctor Command Scripts\n12: \n13: > Documents `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/`. This README uses the `.markdown` extension because\n14: > OpenCode registers every `.md` file under `.skilled/commands/` as a command.\n15: >\n16: > Diagnostic and validation entrypoints used by the doctor command family.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW AND PURPOSE\n21: \n22: `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/` contains shell, JavaScript and Python tools that support doctor routes.\n23: \n24: The scripts validate route manifests, inspect MCP installations, audit parent skill hubs, report graph freshness, check a skill's distance from the Skilled format and convert one, read the root instruction file against the shipped template and embed or restore it, then plan and apply framework release updates. Most are read-only. The release updater's apply and rollback paths write local state, and so does the skill converter's `--apply`. Every script has a test under `tests/`, except `skill-graph-freshness.cjs`, whose freshness panel test lives in the skill advisor runtime. One runner executes them all.\n25: \n26: ---\n27: \n28: ## 2. DIRECTORY TREE\n29: \n30: ```text\n31: .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/\n32: +-- agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs\n33: +-- agents-md-check.cjs\n34: +-- agents-md-embed.cjs\n35: +-- agents-md-manifest.cjs\n36: +-- audit_descriptions.py\n37: +-- command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs\n38: +-- check-mcp-mutation-class.sh\n39: +-- ci-report.cjs\n40: +-- ci-scaffold.cjs\n41: +-- ci-toggle.cjs\n42: +-- doctor-helper-match.cjs\n43: +-- doctor-phrase-reach.cjs\n44: +-- git-hook-gates.cjs\n45: +-- git-setup-check.cjs\n46: +-- git-standards.cjs\n47: +-- mcp-doctor-lib.sh\n48: +-- mcp-doctor.sh\n49: +-- opencode-version-battery.cjs\n50: +-- orphan-process-check.cjs\n51: +-- parent-skill-check.cjs\n52: +-- route-validate.py\n53: +-- route-validate.sh\n54: +-- release-update.cjs\n55: +-- sk-code-quality-config.cjs\n56: +-- sk-code-setup-check.cjs\n57: +-- sk-skill-check.cjs\n58: +-- sk-skill-convert.cjs\n59: +-- skill-graph-freshness.cjs\n60: +-- lib/\n61: |   +-- agents-md-sections.cjs      # the one segmenter, classifier, block reader and delivery measurement the two engines and their suites share\n62: |   +-- ci-profile.cjs             # the repository CI profile loader the report, scaffold and toggle scripts share\n63: |   +-- ci-report-render.cjs       # the report document, its JSON form and its fixed-width table\n64: |   +-- ci-report-sources.cjs      # repository variables, the branch runs come from and the newest non-rebuild commit\n65: |   +-- ci-report-text.cjs         # the one-line rule a gh failure and a YAML parse error both need\n66: |   +-- ci-report-workflows.cjs    # workflow discovery, the YAML parse, switch states and drift flags\n67: |   +-- quality-applied-rules.cjs  # the checklists and override rows the gate would apply, asked through --explain\n68: |   +-- quality-config-init.cjs    # the one mode that writes, from the shipped template with --apply\n69: |   +-- quality-config-source.cjs  # the config source, its trust state and the hygiene rules it switches off\n70: |   +-- quality-report-view.cjs    # the read-only status and check modes\n71: |   +-- quality-script-io.cjs      # the request error and the one place a report reaches stdout\n72: |   +-- quality-skill-paths.cjs    # the one sk-code quality folder and the handles every mode reads\n73: |   +-- sk-skill-convert-transforms.cjs # the pure frontmatter, router-move and legacy-router text transforms\n74: |   `-- sk-skill-surface.cjs       # shared probes the check engine and the converter both call: locate, classify, collisions, spawn\n75: +-- tests/            # one suite per script, plus run-all.sh\n76: |   `-- opencode-version-battery.test.cjs\n77: `-- README.markdown\n78: ```\n79: \n80: ---\n81: \n82: ## 3. KEY FILES\n83: \n84: | File | Responsibility |\n85: |---|---|\n86: | `route-validate.sh` | Runs route-manifest validation and fixture-based self-tests. |\n87: | `route-validate.py` | Implements route schema, asset, tool, mutation and display-parity checks. |\n88: | `mcp-doctor.sh` | Diagnoses supported MCP servers and runtime configuration wiring. |\n89: | `mcp-doctor-lib.sh` | Supplies logging, result tracking, JSON output and configuration helpers to `mcp-doctor.sh`. |\n90: | `check-mcp-mutation-class.sh` | Enforces read-only and mutating classifications for MCP doctor and installer scripts. |\n91: | `parent-skill-check.cjs` | Audits parent skill hubs against structural and routing invariants. |\n92: | `release-update.cjs` | Plans, aligns, applies, rolls back and records framework release bases for the five `/doctor:update` workflows. |\n93: | `skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | Compares compiled, SQLite and on-disk skill graph representations without writing. |\n94: | `sk-skill-check.cjs` | The `/doctor:sk-skill check` engine. With no argument it prints one row per direct child of `.skilled/skills`, and with a skill name or a path one line per row `C1` to `C13`, each carrying its own status and detail. A skill inside another repository's `.skilled/skills` is read against that repository, and each of its rows names the repository it read. A tool that repository does not ship runs from the checker's own copy, and the row names that copy too. It calls the format gate, the root-metadata gate, the derived-block regeneration in dry-run form, the router-equivalence replay, the advisor graph and mirror checks and the description audit, and writes nothing. `--into-hub <hub>` prints the eleven surfaces a skill or mode must land on under that hub instead, each with the probe it ran, and still writes nothing. `--json` prints either report and nothing else. It exits 0 when no row is a finding, 1 when one is, and 2 when a precondition it needs is missing, with the provision command on its own line. |\n95: | `sk-skill-convert.cjs` | Plans, and on request applies, the conversion of one skill. `plan` reads, refuses and prints every step, `apply` prints the same plan and writes only with `--apply`, and `preserve --snapshot <dir>` proves a reshaped body kept every original line. An older Skilled skill converts in place, with its machine router moved verbatim into a new sibling `ROUTER.md`. A plain skill reached through a path outside the skills root is staged under its frontmatter name and becomes a class S standalone root, and it converts only once the operator approves at least eight intent phrases, which become its `intent_signals`. A skill of neither shape is out of scope. Preflight refuses a hub declaration, a nested packet tree, a `local-` or `agent-` prefixed name, a name collision and a description over 130 characters. A skill that declares no `allowed-tools` field prints `allowed-tools: approval required; propose [...]` in its plan, and the apply writes no grant until a list is passed. A field declared twice is refused as `ALLOWED_TOOLS_DUPLICATE` rather than merged. The plain path keeps its agent output, its preservation snapshot and its staging copy in one scratch root beside the backup at `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/scratch/`, deleted when a question is declined and once the run has reported. A skill whose router already lives in `ROUTER.md` gets its frontmatter normalized and nothing else. Every write is preceded by a backup under `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/`, `ROUTER.md` is created exclusively, and a parsed router that would change restores the backup. After both format gates it runs the integration rows: the advisor scan, the compiled skill graph, the reach probe with the budget row, the regression cases and the changelog file and link. |\n96: | `sk-code-setup-check.cjs` | Runs the sk-code setup battery from a repository root: one verdict per setup leg, one repair command per failing leg and no writes. Its quality leg runs the quality runner's own tests, which build throwaway repositories under the OS temp folder and remove them. |\n97: | `sk-code-quality-config.cjs` | The quality config doctor's entry, kept thin: the arguments, the run context, the one call that starts the runner, the mode table and the exit status are here, and each mode sits in the quality modules under `lib/`. It reports which quality config the sk-code quality gate reads, whether each tool it names is on PATH, and the trust state the runner applies to that config: `trusted`, `untrusted` or `none`. `status` and `check` both print it, and when the config is untrusted they print `git config --local skilled.trustRepoChecks true` as the repair. It prints the hygiene layers, a `Hygiene:` line naming the core rules `commentHygiene.disableRules` switches off and an applied-rules block built by calling `quality-gate.cjs --explain` over a sample of one file per first three folders and extension: each checklist with its seat, its override rows or the reasons its section was refused, each same-level conflict and a `Hygiene rules:` line for the rules in force. A refused section, an unknown rule id or a missing checklist fails the status with `CONFIG_INVALID` or `CHECKLIST_MISSING`, and a conflict is listed without failing it. It validates that config and creates `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` once from the shipped template for `/doctor:sk-code quality`, and in a repository with no `specs/` folder its plan suggests the spec-kit hygiene rules without writing them. It never runs a command the config names. It exits 0 on success, 1 on an invalid or missing config, a refused checklist section or a refused init and 2 on a usage error or outside a git repository. |\n98: | `audit_descriptions.py` | Audits description lengths across skills, commands and agents. |\n99: | `agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs` | Verifies every canonical agent reaches all five runtime surfaces, and that mirror surfaces stay symlinked rather than forked. `--check` is accepted and ignored, because it is read-only either way. |\n100: | `agents-md-check.cjs` | The read-only AGENTS.md check: twelve rows reporting the install state, the sections lost or reordered, the currency of each body, every pin, the delivery budget, the shape of the repository block, the repository's own content and its local edits, each runtime copy, the `REPO RULES.md` router and the source template's purity. Three of them run their owner as a child instead of re-deriving it, carrying the rule-copy canary's budget lines, the gate 1 pointer sync's Cursor block and the repo-rules checker's summary. `--root <dir>` classifies another repository and `--json` prints the same report for a machine. A row it cannot prove reads `UNKNOWN` rather than `PASS`, so it exits 0 on no finding, 1 on a finding or an unproven row and 2 on a failed precondition. |\n101: | `agents-md-embed.cjs` | The writing half of `/doctor:agents-md`, in three subcommands. `plan` composes every target in memory and prints the targets, the diffs, the new block, the byte budget, the preservation ledger, the backup folder and the plan digest, and writes nothing. `apply --plan-digest <digest>` recomposes, refuses a digest the tree no longer produces, backs up every path it will touch, writes only inside the allowlist it checks in code and then proves the composition against the bytes on disk, restoring from the backup and ending `STATUS=FAIL` when one line went missing. `restore --run=<dir>` puts back exactly the paths that run's `manifest.json` records, removes the files, and the empty folders, that run created, and keeps the backup. It exits 0 when there was nothing to do or the work finished, 1 on a failed proof or a declined run and 2 on a failed precondition or a path outside the allowlist. |\n102: | `agents-md-manifest.cjs` | Generates and gates the shipped manifest's `derived` key: a copy of every shipped body, its hash and the router rows, taken from the root documents. `--write` refreshes it and `--check` is the freshness gate, so a body that drifted from the template fails here instead of surfacing as a check finding. It refuses a heading no authored entry matches, an authored entry that matches nothing, and text above the first heading, and `--write` runs only in the repository that ships the template. Each of the three engines has its own suite: `agents-md-check.test.cjs`, `agents-md-embed.test.cjs` and `agents-md-manifest.test.cjs`, driven from the recipe folders under `tests/fixtures/agents-md/`. |\n103: | `git-hook-gates.cjs` | Lists the shipped hook gates from `scripts/git-hooks/lib/gates.tsv` with their saved `speckit.hooks.<key>` values, and sets or unsets one in local or global git config for `/doctor:git hooks`. Refuses the per-push approvals. |\n104: | `git-standards.cjs` | Shows where a repository's commit, PR and branch rules come from, copies the shipped sk-git templates into `.sk-git/`, and changes a rules block setting or removes a kind's rules section in those copies for `/doctor:git standards`, re-validating each change with sk-git's own contract check. |\n105: | `git-setup-check.cjs` | Runs the read-only sk-git setup battery for `/doctor:git setup`: eleven checks, each PASS, FAIL, SKIP, UNKNOWN or DEGRADED with the source it read and one repair per failure. It exits 0 when every check passes or skips, 3 when none failed but a check is UNKNOWN or DEGRADED, 1 on any failure and 2 on a usage error. It loads every helper from its own install root and never runs code from the repository it inspects. |\n106: | `command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs` | Compares every command catalog and hub metadata entry back to the command's own frontmatter, which is the one copy nothing else derives from. Structural drift fails. Prose divergence reports and fails only under `--strict`. `--check` is accepted and ignored, because it is read-only either way. |\n107: | `doctor-helper-match.cjs` | Ranks the doctor commands of `_routes.yaml` against a problem stated in plain words for `/doctor:helper`: one entry per route, standalone command, standalone action and mcp sub-action, scored by inverse-document-frequency overlap over the manifest phrases plus a bonus for a fully covered phrase, with a preference for the mutation class the operator asked for. Each candidate carries the manifest phrase that best matched and the terms it matched, so the reason on every line is evidence. It skips its own route and every private name, reads one YAML file and writes nothing. It exits 0 on a match, 1 on no candidate above the floor and 2 on a bad argument, an unreadable manifest or a missing YAML parser. |\n108: | `doctor-phrase-reach.cjs` | Scores every activated doctor phrase and each runnable doctor command through the built advisor scorer, then keeps the three results the hooks show. It fails when a phrase reaches neither the top pick nor the second line. It also fails if a runnable command does not rank first or an activated phrase has fewer than three words. It exits 2 when the advisor build is missing or stale. |\n109: | `opencode-version-battery.cjs` | Checks this repository's OpenCode plugins on OpenCode 1 and 2. On 1 it proves plugin discovery through `debug config` and plugin load through an import check. On 2 it starts and stops a private server with the v2 binary it selected (`$OPENCODE_BIN`, `opencode` or `opencode2`, whichever reports a v2 version) and reads plugin and skill state from it. It exits 0 when every requested leg passes, 1 on a failed leg, 2 on a usage error, a crash or an `--out` file it cannot write and 3 when a leg is degraded or skipped. |\n110: | `ci-report.cjs` | The `/doctor:ci report` entry, kept thin: the flags, the exit codes and every name `/doctor:ci toggle` reads live here, and the readings live in the `ci-report` modules under `lib/`. It reads every workflow under `.github/workflows/` and reports its switch state, triggers, path filters and drift flags for `/doctor:ci report`. A switch is any variable a job's `if:` compares against `off`, so the workflow file is its only copy, and a repository variable value is reduced to a state and never printed. The branch, the required switch, the exempt files, the README and the rebuild trailer come from the CI profile, and the JSON names the profile it read. It exits 0 on a printed report, 1 when a workflow fails to parse, 2 on a bad argument and 3 when the CI profile is missing or invalid. |\n111: | `ci-scaffold.cjs` | Renders the sk-code-ci workflow or job template from the CI profile for `/doctor:ci scaffold`, and with `--protected` prints the profile's protected workflow names. It writes nothing. |\n112: | `ci-toggle.cjs` | Plans one switch change for `/doctor:ci toggle`: the allowlist, the refusals from the live ruleset, `neverOff` and the README, the no-op check, the exact `gh variable` command and the second named confirmation for a protected workflow. It never changes a variable. |\n113: | `lib/ci-profile.cjs` | Finds, validates and normalizes the repository CI profile (`--profile`, then `git config skcode.ciProfile`, then `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/repository/ci.json`, then a generic default that forces nothing), rejecting an unknown key. A profile whose `repository` field names another repository is ignored, and one left at `.sk-code/ci.json` is refused. |\n114: | `lib/ci-report-sources.cjs` | Reads what the report takes from outside the workflow files: the repository variables through `gh variable list`, the branch the repository integrates on and the newest commit on that branch that is not a trigger-index rebuild. Arguments go in as an array and never through a shell, a variable value is reduced to a state and never printed, and a read that fails is reported as unknown with its reason. |\n115: | `lib/ci-report-workflows.cjs` | Reads the workflow files themselves: discovery under `.github/workflows/`, the YAML parse, the switch state per job, the drift flags, the purpose column read from the workflows README and the switch names the report lists. A switch is recognized by its shape, so any repository's switch names are read and none is assumed. |\n116: | `lib/ci-report-render.cjs` | Builds the report document and its two renderings, the JSON a machine reads and the fixed-width table a person reads, both from the same readings. The not-checked list travels with the document, so a clean row is never read as a pass for a rule no parser checks. |\n117: | `lib/ci-report-text.cjs` | `firstLine()`, the one-line rule a `gh` failure and a YAML parse error both need, because a whole message would break the table row, the JSON field and the stderr line that carry it. |\n118: | `lib/quality-skill-paths.cjs` | The one sk-code quality folder this doctor reads, and the handles inside it that answer every mode's question. Each path resolves from the file's own location, so the doctor works from a checkout anywhere, and the runner, the config reader and the helpers load here so every mode reads one skill folder, one config schema and the one runner the gate itself starts. |\n119: | `lib/quality-script-io.cjs` | The two ways the quality doctor answers whoever started it: the typed request failure that carries the exit code and the `STATUS=` error code a route reads, and `emit()`, the single place a report reaches stdout. One function writes the last line, so a route reads one whatever the mode. |\n120: | `lib/quality-config-source.cjs` | Which repository config a mode read, whether the runner trusts it and which core hygiene rules it switches off, every answer read through the module the gate itself reads so a doctor verdict and a gate verdict cannot drift. It also builds the suggestion an init prints for a repository with no `specs/` folder. |\n121: | `lib/quality-config-init.cjs` | The one mode that can write: it shows the config it would create from the shipped template and writes it only with `--apply`, through a hard link from a temporary file, so it never replaces a config that already exists. An existing config is refused rather than merged. |\n122: | `lib/quality-report-view.cjs` | The two read-only modes. `status` reports the config, its trust, the tools it names, the hygiene layers and what the gate would apply here, and `check` stops at the config itself. Both render the lines a route prints and the document `--json` carries from one set of readings, and neither starts a configured check. |\n123: | `lib/quality-applied-rules.cjs` | Which checklists and hygiene rules the gate would apply to a repository and which override rows inside them it would honour, asked of the runner itself through `--explain` over a sampled file per folder group and extension, so the doctor keeps no second copy of checklist discovery or of the override grammar. |\n124: | `lib/sk-skill-surface.cjs` | The probes `/doctor:sk-skill` shares between its check engine and its converter: resolve a name or a path to a skill root, classify where that root sits (a direct child of `.skilled/skills`, outside it or nested in a hub), read the frontmatter fields, inspect name collisions against the reserved and persona prefixes, and wrap a spawned validator so its JSON, its exit status and its missing-tool cases read the same in both scripts. |\n125: | `lib/sk-skill-convert-transforms.cjs` | The pure text transforms the converter applies, kept free of the repository so they are unit-tested on strings: normalize the `version` and `allowed-tools` frontmatter, find the machine blocks a Smart Routing section still holds, move a legacy trailing machine router section into the sibling router template, and add the router link line. |\n126: | `orphan-process-check.cjs` | Lists the helper processes and model servers this user started that were reparented to init and are older than a minimum age (60 minutes by default), classifying each as a node test, vitest, tail monitor, grep search, executor worker, session-relaunch test helper, model server or git-live-follow, and reports each one's age and memory with a total. `--reap` sends `SIGTERM` after re-checking each process's identity, `--yes` confirms it, and `--json` and `--summary` print machine-readable and one-line forms. It exits 0 on a printed report, 1 on an unexpected error and 2 on a bad argument. |\n127: \n128: ---\n129: \n130: ## 4. ROUTE VALIDATION\n131: \n132: `route-validate.sh` delegates the live manifest checks to `route-validate.py`.\n133: \n134: The validator checks:\n135: \n136: - Manifest parsing and schema version\n137: - Required route fields\n138: - Unique targets\n139: - Referenced YAML assets\n140: - Mutation classes\n141: - MCP tool grants\n142: - Trigger phrases\n143: - Script path resolution\n144: - Each route names an existing doctor router\n145: - Target parity between each command's router and presentation\n146: - Read-only route mutation policy\n147: - Workflow activity coverage for every route script invocation\n148: \n149: Run it from the repository root:\n150: \n151: ```bash\n152: bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/route-validate.sh\n153: ```\n154: \n155: Run its negative fixture suite:\n156: \n157: ```bash\n158: bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/route-validate.sh --self-test\n159: ```\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 5. DIAGNOSTIC ENTRYPOINTS\n164: \n165: | Command | Purpose |\n166: |---|---|\n167: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-check.cjs [--root <dir>] [--json]` | Report the twelve AGENTS.md rows for this repository or another one, and write nothing. |\n168: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-embed.cjs plan [--root <dir>] [--keep-claude-md] [--create-router]` | Print every target with its diff, the new block, the budget, the preservation ledger, the backup folder and the plan digest. Writes nothing, and prints `NOTHING TO DO` when the repository is already in its target state. |\n169: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-embed.cjs apply --plan-digest <digest> [--root <dir>]` | Back up, write inside the allowlist and prove the composition, only with the digest the plan printed. |\n170: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-embed.cjs restore --run=<dir> --yes` | Put back exactly the paths one run recorded, and remove the files, and the empty folders, that run created. |\n171: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-manifest.cjs --check` | Fail when the manifest's derived key no longer matches the shipped documents. |\n172: | `bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/mcp-doctor.sh` | Diagnose all supported MCP servers. |\n173: | `bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/mcp-doctor.sh --json` | Emit machine-readable MCP diagnostics. |\n174: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/parent-skill-check.cjs <skill-dir>` | Audit one parent skill hub. |\n175: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | Report skill graph drift. |\n176: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-check.cjs check [<name-or-path>] [--json] [--into-hub <hub>]` | Report how far one skill, or the whole fleet, is from the Skilled format row by row, or report a skill's gaps against a hub's eleven surfaces. |\n177: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-convert.cjs plan <name-or-path>` | Print the conversion plan for one skill and write nothing. A plain skill refuses until at least eight `--approved-phrase` values are passed, and a skill with no `allowed-tools` field prints the list its plan proposes. |\n178: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-convert.cjs apply <name-or-path> --apply` | Write a plan already shown and approved: normalize the frontmatter and move a legacy machine router into `ROUTER.md`, or stage a plain skill as a new root. Without `--apply` it prints the plan and stops. `preserve --snapshot <dir>` proves the reshaped body first and writes nothing. |\n179: | `python3 .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/audit_descriptions.py --repo-root .` | Audit description budgets. |\n180: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs` | Report agent-roster coverage drift across runtimes. |\n181: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs` | Report command-catalog and hub-metadata drift. |\n182: | `bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/check-mcp-mutation-class.sh` | Check the read-only and mutating classes of MCP doctor and installer scripts. |\n183: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/release-update.cjs check --offline` | Report what a release update would change, without fetching. |\n184: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/git-hook-gates.cjs list` | Show every hook gate and its saved setting. |\n185: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/git-standards.cjs status` | Show which commit, PR and branch rules are enforced, and from where. |\n186: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/git-setup-check.cjs` | Check which sk-git hooks, rules and settings are live in this repository. |\n187: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-code-quality-config.cjs status` | Show which quality config the gate reads, whether each tool it names is on PATH, the trust state the runner applies to that config, the hygiene rules it switches off and the checklists and override rows the gate would apply. |\n188: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs` | Check this repository's OpenCode plugins on OpenCode 1 and 2. |\n189: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs --family=v1\\|v2\\|both` | Choose the family to check. The default is `both`. |\n190: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs --json` | Print the JSON summary on stdout instead of the human lines. |\n191: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs --root <dir> --out <file>` | Inspect another repository root and write the JSON summary to the named file. |\n192: \n193: The battery runs five legs:\n194: \n195: - `family-present`: PASS when a candidate's `--version` succeeds and parses to that family.\n196: - `plugin-discovered`: PASS when OpenCode 1's `debug config` exits 0 with parseable JSON, the plugin folder holds at least one top-level `.js` file, none is a broken symlink and every one is listed.\n197: - `plugin-load`: PASS when the import check exits 0 and reports every top-level `.js` file loaded.\n198: - `plugin-active`: PASS when two consecutive reads give the same non-zero count and, on that read, every top-level `.js` file has an entry under the inspected root with status active.\n199: - `skill-visible`: PASS when the skill listing parses and has an entry that mentions the inspected root.\n200: - `command-listed`: PASS when every `.md` file under the project's `.opencode/commands/` folder (or its singular `command/` form) is listed under the name it registers, which is its relative path without `.md`, or on OpenCode 1 the `name:` in its leading frontmatter when it has one, and every listed name comes from a folder the runtime reads (the global config folders, and on OpenCode 2 every ancestor `.opencode` folder plus the built-ins `init` and `review`). On OpenCode 2 a `<server>:<prompt>` name also passes when `<server>` is an MCP server in the config sources the private server reports, and a PASS notes `mcp prompts: <n>`. When the config sources did not parse, a `<server>:<prompt>` name in no command folder degrades the leg instead of reading as a stray, because the server list cannot be judged. A listed name that a non-command file in the project's command folder could produce also fails, even when a global or inherited folder holds a command of that name: any file not ending in `.md`, such as `README.markdown`, `.txt` or `.yaml`, counts under its relative path both with and without its extension. The two OpenCode 2 built-ins `init` and `review` and a `<server>:<prompt>` name from a configured MCP server are exempt, because the rule catches an OpenCode version that starts scanning more than `*.md`. A name that a `.md` command in the same folder already owns is left out, because the command answers to that name.\n201: \n202: A family that is not installed is SKIP, never FAIL.\n203: \n204: On this repository all legs pass on OpenCode 1.19.3 and 2.0.32. OpenCode 1.19.3 cuts piped stdout at 65,536 bytes, which is why the collectors read stdout from files.\n205: \n206: ---\n207: \n208: ## 6. MUTATION BOUNDARIES\n209: \n210: | Script | Boundary |\n211: |---|---|\n212: | `route-validate.sh` | Read-only during normal validation. Its self-test creates temporary fixture files and removes them on exit. |\n213: | `mcp-doctor.sh` | Read-only diagnostic. Repairs belong to the `/doctor:mcp` workflows, one approval at a time. Exit 3 means bad arguments. |\n214: | `check-mcp-mutation-class.sh` | Read-only contract scan. |\n215: | `parent-skill-check.cjs` | Read-only audit. |\n216: | `skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | Read-only report. |\n217: | `sk-skill-check.cjs` | Read-only, including the hub gap report: every validator and every hub checker runs in its check-only form and its derived-block probe is a dry run. |\n218: | `sk-skill-convert.cjs` | `plan` and `preserve` are read-only. `apply` is a dry run until `--apply`. An in-place conversion rewrites the skill's `SKILL.md` and creates its sibling `ROUTER.md`, and a plain skill is staged as a new root under `.skilled/skills/<name>/`. Both back the pre-write bytes up under `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/` and never replace a file that exists. The plain path keeps its three temporary folders in one scratch root beside them at `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/scratch/`, and deletes it when a question is declined and again once a finished run has reported. The integration rows then write the advisor graph, the compiled `skill-graph.json` and the regression cases file, and every one of them is listed in the undo block the run prints. |\n219: | `audit_descriptions.py` | Read-only audit. |\n220: | `agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs`, `command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs` | Read-only reports. |\n221: | `release-update.cjs` | `check` and `align --dry-run` are read-only. `align` writes a run directory. `apply`, `rollback` and `record-base` write framework files and release records under a lock. The `unlock` subcommand removes a lock only when its owner process is gone. |\n222: \n223: | `git-hook-gates.cjs` | `list` is read-only. `set` is a dry run until `--apply`, which writes only `speckit.hooks.<key>` in local or global git config. |\n224: | `git-standards.cjs` | `status` and `check` are read-only. `init`, `set` and `disable` are dry runs until `--apply`, which writes only `.sk-git/` or the `skgit.contractDir` directory, never the shipped sk-git templates. |\n225: | `git-setup-check.cjs` | Read-only. It writes no file and no git config value, and calls no allocator or `--apply` mode. |\n226: | `sk-code-quality-config.cjs` | `status` and `check` are read-only; `status` starts the runner's `--explain` once per batch of up to 500 sampled paths and no configured check. `init` is a dry run until `--apply`, which creates only `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` and refuses with `CONFIG_EXISTS` when that file exists. |\n227: | `agents-md-check.cjs` | Read-only over any root: it reads the instruction file, its runtime copies and `REPO RULES.md`, runs three owner tools as children in their check-only forms and writes nothing. |\n228: | `agents-md-embed.cjs` | `plan` is read-only. `apply` writes only the paths in its own allowlist, all of them under the target root, after backing each one up under `.skilled/output/doctor-agents-md/<digest>/` (or the OS temp folder when that path is not ignored), and restores from that backup itself when its post-write proof fails. `restore` writes only the paths the run's `manifest.json` records and keeps the backup folder. It never runs `git add`, `git commit` or `git stash`. |\n229: | `agents-md-manifest.cjs` | `--check` is read-only. `--write` rewrites the manifest's `derived` key and refuses to run outside the repository that ships the template. |\n230: | `opencode-version-battery.cjs` | The battery itself writes only the `--out` file and temporary folders it removes when each step ends. The private server's folder is also removed on `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM`, and the server is stopped. The import check runs each plugin's v1 factory in a child process rooted at the repository, so anything a plugin does when it loads happens during the check. It never touches the shared v2 service and never prints the private server's password. OpenCode 1 writes into a temporary data folder, not the user's. |\n231: \n232: Do not invoke a mutating path from a route classified as read-only.\n233: \n234: ---\n235: \n236: ## 7. VALIDATION AND INSTRUCTIONS\n237: \n238: Run every doctor test from the repository root. CI runs the same command in the `doctor-scripts` job of `.github/workflows/spec-kit-check.yml`:\n239: \n240: ```bash\n241: bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/tests/run-all.sh\n242: ```\n243: \n244: The runner needs `node`, `python3` with PyYAML, and the advisor runtime's dependencies (`npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime ci`). It exits `0` when every suite passes, `1` when one fails and `2` when a required tool is missing. `tests/README.markdown` lists the suites.\n245: \n246: Each script can be pointed at a fixture tree, which is how the tests run without touching the repository:\n247: \n248: | Script | Override |\n249: |---|---|\n250: | `agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs`, `command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs`, `mcp-doctor.sh`, `release-update.cjs` | `--root <dir>` |\n251: | `agents-md-check.cjs` | `--root <dir>`. It also takes `--manifest <path>` and `--source` |\n252: | `git-hook-gates.cjs`, `git-standards.cjs`, `git-setup-check.cjs`, `sk-code-quality-config.cjs` | `--repo <dir>`. `git-hook-gates.cjs` also reads `GATE_REGISTRY`, and `git-setup-check.cjs` also takes `--tool <name>` |\n253: | `audit_descriptions.py`, `route-validate.sh` | `--repo-root <dir>`. `route-validate.sh` also reads `REPO_ROOT`, `ROUTES_FILE`, `DOCTOR_DIR` and `ASSETS_DIR` |\n254: | `check-mcp-mutation-class.sh` | the repository root as its first argument |\n255: | `parent-skill-check.cjs` | `PARENT_HUB_CHECK_COMMANDS_DIR` for the commands tree. `PARENT_HUB_CHECK_STRICT=0` reports advisory findings as warnings, and hard invariants still fail |\n256: | `skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | `SKILL_GRAPH_FRESHNESS_ROOT`, `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DB_DIR` |\n257: \n258: ---\n259: \n260: ## 8. RELATED\n261: \n262: - [Doctor route manifest](../../../commands/doctor/_routes.yaml)\n263: - [Doctor command routers](../../../commands/doctor/)\n264: - [Doctor assets](../../../commands/doctor/assets/)\n265: - [Doctor script tests](./tests/README.markdown)\n266: - [Commands directory](../../../commands/)"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown\"}"},"time":{"start":1794528518905,"end":1794528520032}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794528520036,"sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","part":{"partID":"prt_1d23c2619001vRT7neChEhPANu","sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","messageID":"msg_1d23c0a6e0019tmQfbw4yQlp70","type":"tool","id":"call_7910173f22c1431c84e7d30c","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/hook-wiring-check.cjs"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/hook-wiring-check.cjs, lines 1-161\n1: #!/usr/bin/env node\n2: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n3: // MODULE: Hook Wiring Check\n4: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n5: 'use strict';\n6: \n7: // Read-only /doctor diagnostic for the prompt hook entry each runtime loads.\n8: //\n9: // WHY THIS EXISTS: the skill advisor reaches a first prompt only through the hook\n10: // entry a runtime fires before the model sees that prompt. A runtime folder can be\n11: // installed while its hook file is missing, its JSON is unreadable, or the entry\n12: // was never added, and then the advisor is silent with no error at session start.\n13: // This check names the runtime and the file to repair.\n14: //\n15: // Never writes. Exit 0 when every installed runtime carries its entry, 1 when one\n16: // is missing, 2 on a bad argument or a missing root.\n17: \n18: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n19: // 1. IMPORTS\n20: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n21: \n22: const fs = require('node:fs');\n23: const path = require('node:path');\n24: \n25: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n26: // 2. CONSTANTS\n27: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n28: \n29: const USAGE = [\n30:   'Usage: hook-wiring-check.cjs --root <dir> [--json]',\n31:   '  --root  check the checkout under <dir>.',\n32:   '  --json  print one JSON object instead of one line per runtime.',\n33: ].join('\\n');\n34: \n35: // Each entry names the runtime folder that decides whether the check applies,\n36: // the hook file inside it, the event that carries the prompt entry, and the path\n37: // fragment whose presence proves the entry is wired. A runtime folder that is\n38: // absent is skipped, because that runtime was never installed in this checkout.\n39: const RUNTIMES = [\n40:   {\n41:     id: 'claude',\n42:     dir: '.claude',\n43:     file: '.claude/settings.json',\n44:     event: 'UserPromptSubmit',\n45:     marker: 'hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js',\n46:   },\n47:   {\n48:     id: 'codex',\n49:     dir: '.codex',\n50:     file: '.codex/hooks.json',\n51:     event: 'UserPromptSubmit',\n52:     marker: 'hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js',\n53:   },\n54:   {\n55:     id: 'cursor',\n56:     dir: '.cursor',\n57:     file: '.cursor/hooks.json',\n58:     event: 'beforeSubmitPrompt',\n59:     marker: 'hooks/cursor/user-prompt-submit.js',\n60:   },\n61:   {\n62:     id: 'devin',\n63:     dir: '.devin',\n64:     file: '.devin/hooks.v1.json',\n65:     event: 'UserPromptSubmit',\n66:     marker: 'hooks/devin/user-prompt-submit.js',\n67:   },\n68: ];\n69: \n70: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n71: // 3. HELPERS\n72: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n73: \n74: function fail(message) {\n75:   process.stderr.write(`hook-wiring-check: ${message}\\n`);\n76:   process.exit(2);\n77: }\n78: \n79: function parseArgs(argv) {\n80:   const opts = { root: null, json: false };\n81:   for (let i = 0; i < argv.length; i += 1) {\n82:     const arg = argv[i];\n83:     if (arg === '--json') {\n84:       opts.json = true;\n85:     } else if (arg === '--root') {\n86:       const value = argv[i + 1];\n87:       if (!value || value.startsWith('-')) fail(`--root needs a directory\\n${USAGE}`);\n88:       opts.root = path.resolve(value);\n89:       i += 1;\n90:     } else {\n91:       fail(`unknown argument: ${arg}\\n${USAGE}`);\n92:     }\n93:   }\n94:   if (!opts.root) fail(`--root is required\\n${USAGE}`);\n95:   if (!fs.existsSync(opts.root) || !fs.statSync(opts.root).isDirectory()) {\n96:     fail(`root not found: ${opts.root}`);\n97:   }\n98:   return opts;\n99: }\n100: \n101: // Hook files either wrap their events in a `hooks` object or place them at the\n102: // top level, and the cursor file omits the nested hook array the others carry.\n103: // Searching the event payload's JSON text covers all three shapes without\n104: // teaching this check each dialect.\n105: function eventPayload(document, event) {\n106:   if (!document || typeof document !== 'object') return undefined;\n107:   if (document.hooks && typeof document.hooks === 'object' && event in document.hooks) {\n108:     return document.hooks[event];\n109:   }\n110:   if (event in document) return document[event];\n111:   return undefined;\n112: }\n113: \n114: function inspect(runtime, root) {\n115:   const file = path.join(root, runtime.file);\n116:   let document;\n117:   try {\n118:     document = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(file, 'utf8'));\n119:   } catch {\n120:     return { runtime: runtime.id, file: runtime.file, status: 'hook-missing' };\n121:   }\n122:   const payload = eventPayload(document, runtime.event);\n123:   const wired = payload !== undefined && JSON.stringify(payload).includes(runtime.marker);\n124:   return { runtime: runtime.id, file: runtime.file, status: wired ? 'ok' : 'hook-missing' };\n125: }\n126: \n127: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n128: // 4. MAIN\n129: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n130: \n131: function main(argv) {\n132:   const opts = parseArgs(argv);\n133:   const checks = RUNTIMES.map((runtime) => {\n134:     if (!fs.existsSync(path.join(opts.root, runtime.dir))) {\n135:       return { runtime: runtime.id, file: runtime.file, status: 'skipped' };\n136:     }\n137:     return inspect(runtime, opts.root);\n138:   });\n139: \n140:   if (opts.json) {\n141:     process.stdout.write(`${JSON.stringify({ root: opts.root, checks }, null, 2)}\\n`);\n142:   } else {\n143:     for (const check of checks) {\n144:       if (check.status === 'ok') process.stdout.write(`ok ${check.runtime}\\n`);\n145:       else if (check.status === 'hook-missing') process.stdout.write(`hook-missing ${check.runtime} ${check.file}\\n`);\n146:     }\n147:   }\n148: \n149:   return checks.some((check) => check.status === 'hook-missing') ? 1 : 0;\n150: }\n151: \n152: if (require.main === module) {\n153:   try {\n154:     process.exitCode = main(process.argv.slice(2));\n155:   } catch (err) {\n156:     process.stderr.write(`hook-wiring-check: ${err && err.message ? err.message : String(err)}\\n`);\n157:     process.exitCode = 2;\n158:   }\n159: }\n160: \n161: module.exports = { main, eventPayload };","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/hook-wiring-check.cjs, lines 1-161\n1: #!/usr/bin/env node\n2: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n3: // MODULE: Hook Wiring Check\n4: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n5: 'use strict';\n6: \n7: // Read-only /doctor diagnostic for the prompt hook entry each runtime loads.\n8: //\n9: // WHY THIS EXISTS: the skill advisor reaches a first prompt only through the hook\n10: // entry a runtime fires before the model sees that prompt. A runtime folder can be\n11: // installed while its hook file is missing, its JSON is unreadable, or the entry\n12: // was never added, and then the advisor is silent with no error at session start.\n13: // This check names the runtime and the file to repair.\n14: //\n15: // Never writes. Exit 0 when every installed runtime carries its entry, 1 when one\n16: // is missing, 2 on a bad argument or a missing root.\n17: \n18: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n19: // 1. IMPORTS\n20: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n21: \n22: const fs = require('node:fs');\n23: const path = require('node:path');\n24: \n25: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n26: // 2. CONSTANTS\n27: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n28: \n29: const USAGE = [\n30:   'Usage: hook-wiring-check.cjs --root <dir> [--json]',\n31:   '  --root  check the checkout under <dir>.',\n32:   '  --json  print one JSON object instead of one line per runtime.',\n33: ].join('\\n');\n34: \n35: // Each entry names the runtime folder that decides whether the check applies,\n36: // the hook file inside it, the event that carries the prompt entry, and the path\n37: // fragment whose presence proves the entry is wired. A runtime folder that is\n38: // absent is skipped, because that runtime was never installed in this checkout.\n39: const RUNTIMES = [\n40:   {\n41:     id: 'claude',\n42:     dir: '.claude',\n43:     file: '.claude/settings.json',\n44:     event: 'UserPromptSubmit',\n45:     marker: 'hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js',\n46:   },\n47:   {\n48:     id: 'codex',\n49:     dir: '.codex',\n50:     file: '.codex/hooks.json',\n51:     event: 'UserPromptSubmit',\n52:     marker: 'hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js',\n53:   },\n54:   {\n55:     id: 'cursor',\n56:     dir: '.cursor',\n57:     file: '.cursor/hooks.json',\n58:     event: 'beforeSubmitPrompt',\n59:     marker: 'hooks/cursor/user-prompt-submit.js',\n60:   },\n61:   {\n62:     id: 'devin',\n63:     dir: '.devin',\n64:     file: '.devin/hooks.v1.json',\n65:     event: 'UserPromptSubmit',\n66:     marker: 'hooks/devin/user-prompt-submit.js',\n67:   },\n68: ];\n69: \n70: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n71: // 3. HELPERS\n72: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n73: \n74: function fail(message) {\n75:   process.stderr.write(`hook-wiring-check: ${message}\\n`);\n76:   process.exit(2);\n77: }\n78: \n79: function parseArgs(argv) {\n80:   const opts = { root: null, json: false };\n81:   for (let i = 0; i < argv.length; i += 1) {\n82:     const arg = argv[i];\n83:     if (arg === '--json') {\n84:       opts.json = true;\n85:     } else if (arg === '--root') {\n86:       const value = argv[i + 1];\n87:       if (!value || value.startsWith('-')) fail(`--root needs a directory\\n${USAGE}`);\n88:       opts.root = path.resolve(value);\n89:       i += 1;\n90:     } else {\n91:       fail(`unknown argument: ${arg}\\n${USAGE}`);\n92:     }\n93:   }\n94:   if (!opts.root) fail(`--root is required\\n${USAGE}`);\n95:   if (!fs.existsSync(opts.root) || !fs.statSync(opts.root).isDirectory()) {\n96:     fail(`root not found: ${opts.root}`);\n97:   }\n98:   return opts;\n99: }\n100: \n101: // Hook files either wrap their events in a `hooks` object or place them at the\n102: // top level, and the cursor file omits the nested hook array the others carry.\n103: // Searching the event payload's JSON text covers all three shapes without\n104: // teaching this check each dialect.\n105: function eventPayload(document, event) {\n106:   if (!document || typeof document !== 'object') return undefined;\n107:   if (document.hooks && typeof document.hooks === 'object' && event in document.hooks) {\n108:     return document.hooks[event];\n109:   }\n110:   if (event in document) return document[event];\n111:   return undefined;\n112: }\n113: \n114: function inspect(runtime, root) {\n115:   const file = path.join(root, runtime.file);\n116:   let document;\n117:   try {\n118:     document = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(file, 'utf8'));\n119:   } catch {\n120:     return { runtime: runtime.id, file: runtime.file, status: 'hook-missing' };\n121:   }\n122:   const payload = eventPayload(document, runtime.event);\n123:   const wired = payload !== undefined && JSON.stringify(payload).includes(runtime.marker);\n124:   return { runtime: runtime.id, file: runtime.file, status: wired ? 'ok' : 'hook-missing' };\n125: }\n126: \n127: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n128: // 4. MAIN\n129: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n130: \n131: function main(argv) {\n132:   const opts = parseArgs(argv);\n133:   const checks = RUNTIMES.map((runtime) => {\n134:     if (!fs.existsSync(path.join(opts.root, runtime.dir))) {\n135:       return { runtime: runtime.id, file: runtime.file, status: 'skipped' };\n136:     }\n137:     return inspect(runtime, opts.root);\n138:   });\n139: \n140:   if (opts.json) {\n141:     process.stdout.write(`${JSON.stringify({ root: opts.root, checks }, null, 2)}\\n`);\n142:   } else {\n143:     for (const check of checks) {\n144:       if (check.status === 'ok') process.stdout.write(`ok ${check.runtime}\\n`);\n145:       else if (check.status === 'hook-missing') process.stdout.write(`hook-missing ${check.runtime} ${check.file}\\n`);\n146:     }\n147:   }\n148: \n149:   return checks.some((check) => check.status === 'hook-missing') ? 1 : 0;\n150: }\n151: \n152: if (require.main === module) {\n153:   try {\n154:     process.exitCode = main(process.argv.slice(2));\n155:   } catch (err) {\n156:     process.stderr.write(`hook-wiring-check: ${err && err.message ? err.message : String(err)}\\n`);\n157:     process.exitCode = 2;\n158:   }\n159: }\n160: \n161: module.exports = { main, eventPayload };"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/hook-wiring-check.cjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794528519705,"end":1794528520036}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794528523078,"sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","part":{"id":"prt_1d23c3346001qK058LM3MhjObE","sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","messageID":"msg_1d23c0a6e0019tmQfbw4yQlp70","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"a72e4263dd69df5609f6e164d1d153fde42aeb9b","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":10620,"output":232,"reasoning":175,"cache":{"read":16896,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794528530121,"sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","part":{"id":"prt_1d23c4ec9001DxM7d2rP2S3ugK","sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","messageID":"msg_1d23c334b001dEKejz7dlpD6dn","type":"step-start","snapshot":"0fb032dbcb130a849c3a7c44f65a26faec64fc06"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794528544198,"sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","part":{"id":"prt_1d23c8203001xtiARxJJcLQo96","sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","messageID":"msg_1d23c334b001dEKejz7dlpD6dn","type":"text","text":"Now I have the flags and exit codes for both scripts. Per the repo's gates, let me load repo rules and the doc skill before the write.","time":{"start":1794528543235,"end":1794528544198}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794528544226,"sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","part":{"partID":"prt_1d23c83e7001qk0G5MhFK0hyrp","sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","messageID":"msg_1d23c334b001dEKejz7dlpD6dn","type":"tool","id":"call_70e789b8d0a54fb29d53e43d","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794528543719,"end":1794528544226}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794528544231,"sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","part":{"partID":"prt_1d23c85330013gg41tzZnqcQhd","sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","messageID":"msg_1d23c334b001dEKejz7dlpD6dn","type":"tool","id":"call_100ed3c399754acaa21f355a","tool":"skill","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"id":"sk-doc"},"output":"<skill_content name=\"sk-doc\">\n# Skill: sk-doc\n\n<!-- Keywords: sk-doc, documentation, markdown, authoring, parent-hub, mode-registry, hub-router, workflowmode, packetkind, sk-create-skill, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command, sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark, sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter, sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, sk-create-quality-control, human-voice-rules, hvr, shared-backbone, create-quality-control-pipeline -->\n\n# Documentation Authoring Hub (sk-doc)\n\nOne advisor identity, fifteen workflow modes across fourteen packets, one shared sk-create-quality-control backbone.\n`sk-doc` is the parent hub for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring.\nIt holds NO per-packet logic: it routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each packet keeps its own contract in its nested folder.\nThe cross-cutting sk-create-quality-control pipeline (validators, global standards, frontmatter/llms/template assets) lives once in `shared/` and is consumed by every packet.\n\n**Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`; `description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value, so every hub-root JSON routing file states the same release; `ROUTER.md` keeps its own document version.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring, and for document-quality work. The hub classifies the request, resolves a `workflowMode`, and loads the matching nested packet.\n\n| Mode | Use it for | Packet | Command |\n|------|------------|--------|---------|\n| **sk-create-skill** | Scaffold an OpenCode skill (and, via `sk-create-skill-parent`, a parent hub with nested mode packets) | `sk-create-skill/` | `/create:skill`, `/create:skill-parent` |\n| **sk-create-readme** | Author a folder README, general or code-folder | `sk-create-readme/` | `/create:readme` |\n| **sk-create-agent** | Scaffold an OpenCode agent (permission/authority frontmatter) | `sk-create-agent/` | `/create:agent` |\n| **sk-create-command** | Scaffold an OpenCode slash command (argument-hint + allowed-tools + router/presentation split) | `sk-create-command/` | `/create:command` |\n| **sk-create-feature-catalog** | Author a feature-catalog inventory package | `sk-create-feature-catalog/` | `/create:feature-catalog` |\n| **sk-create-manual-testing-playbook** | Author a manual-testing-playbook package | `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/` | `/create:manual-testing-playbook` |\n| **sk-create-benchmark** | Author MCP-promotion and behavior packages or inputs | `sk-create-benchmark/` | `/create:benchmark` |\n| **sk-create-changelog** | Author a global component changelog entry (version bump + topology-aware placement). Packet-local changelogs are deprecated | `sk-create-changelog/` | `/create:changelog` |\n| **sk-create-diff** | Produce a local, Git-free before/after review of an edited document (text/Markdown/HTML/DOCX/text-PDF) as a self-contained HTML report | `sk-create-diff/` | `/create:diff` |\n| **sk-create-repo-rule** | Create, revise or retire a repo-local rule under `repo-rules/` and wire it into `REPO RULES.md` (four decision tests refuse most requests) | `sk-create-repo-rule/` | `/create:repo-rule` |\n| **sk-create-with-human-voice** | Apply the Human Voice Rules to prose, or score prose against them, with a scope gate and a re-scan | `sk-create-with-human-voice/` | `/create:with-human-voice` |\n| **sk-create-frontmatter** | Own the YAML frontmatter contract: per-class field rules, the description budget, and the 4-part `version` derivation | `sk-create-frontmatter/` | — (routes via aliases) |\n| **sk-create-goal** | Author or revise a packet `goal.md`: the durable directive, three to seven self-contained completion criteria, and the phase-parent binding table (session-goal setting stays with the goal hooks) | `sk-create-goal/` | `/create:goal` |\n| **sk-create-quality-control** | Validate / score / optimize an EXISTING document (extract → DQI → HVR → validate) | `sk-create-quality-control/` | (routes via aliases) |\n\n### When NOT to Use\n- Code implementation, tests, or debugging — use `sk-code`.\n- Git worktree, branch, commit, PR, or release work — use `sk-git`.\n- Spec-folder workflow / memory / save context — use `system-spec-kit`.\n- A measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source) — use `sk-design-md-generator`.\n- The `shared/` backbone is consumed by the packets, not invoked as a user workflow.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nRouting is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\nStage 1 (hub → mode) selects a mode: `hub-router.json` `vocabularyClasses` is the one authored routing vocabulary.\nThe `mode-registry.json` aliases, each packet's `Keyword triggers:` line and the root `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against it.\n`sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-hub-vocabulary-source.cjs` fails on the `alias`, `stage-two`, `triggers` and `packets` legs; its `stage-two-reverse` leg only reports.\nStage 2 (mode → leaves) lives in the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md), which maps the request's authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources that mode loads.\nThe two layers stay separate: the hub never emits leaf paths, and the surface router never re-decides the mode.\nThe advisor routes every documentation and authoring query to the single identity `sk-doc`.\nResolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-doc --prompt \"<task>\"`.\nAct on the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\nCompiled routing is the default.\nAn unclear documentation intent asks for disambiguation, because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\nRun the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Terms\n\n- **`workflowMode`**: the public packet key a request resolves to, such as `sk-create-skill`.\n- **`packetKind`**: `workflow` for every sk-doc packet, because this hub has no surface axis.\n- **`backendKind`**: `template-scaffold` for the create-* generators and `sk-create-quality-control` for the quality-control mode.\n- **packet**: a nested `sk-create-*` folder that keeps its own authoring contract, references, assets and scripts.\n- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each packet's discriminator, aliases, command and `advisorRouting`.\n- **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's signals, vocabulary classes, default fallback resource and bundle rules.\n- **`vocabularyClasses`**: the one authored routing vocabulary that the registry aliases, the `Keyword triggers:` lines and the `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against.\n- **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, which map an authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources its mode loads.\n- **leaf**: a canonical `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n- **`defer`**: the outcome that asks for disambiguation, which this hub reaches because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\n- **shared backbone**: the `shared/` copy of the sk-create-quality-control pipeline that every packet consumes.\n- **`graph-metadata.json`**: the one advisor identity for the whole skill, which no packet or `shared/` directory carries.\n\n### Layout\n```\nsk-doc/\n  SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-packet logic)\n  ROUTER.md              # stage-two surface router (authoring intent -> leaf sets)\n  mode-registry.json     # the fifteen-mode discriminator + advisorRouting (single source of truth)\n  hub-router.json        # router signals + vocabulary classes\n  description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n  graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n  changelog/  manual-testing-playbook/  benchmark/  feature-catalog/\n  sk-create-skill/  sk-create-readme/  sk-create-agent/  sk-create-command/\n  sk-create-feature-catalog/  sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/\n  sk-create-benchmark/  sk-create-changelog/  sk-create-diff/\n  sk-create-repo-rule/  sk-create-frontmatter/  sk-create-goal/\n  sk-create-with-human-voice/  sk-create-quality-control/    # nested workflow packets\n  scripts/               # facade symlinks -> shared/ + owning packets (tool paths only)\n  shared/                # sk-create-quality-control backbone: validators, global standards, shared assets\n```\n\nEach packet is self-contained (its own `SKILL.md`, `README.md`, `changelog/`, and moved `references/`/`assets/`/`scripts/`) and carries **no** `graph-metadata.json`, so the advisor discovers exactly one `sk-doc` identity.\n\n### Shared backbone\n`shared/` holds the universal sk-create-quality-control pipeline consumed by every packet:\n- Generic validator scripts in `shared/scripts/`.\n- Cross-cutting standards and vocabulary in `shared/references/`.\n- Shared templates in `shared/assets/`.\n\nThe generic validator `shared/scripts/cite-drift-scan.mjs` counts and resolves the `<path>.<ext>:<line>` citations in every tracked skill doc.\nIt makes zero model calls by default.\n`--jev` runs the backend and needs `--out <dir>`, so every call is recorded, and the Jev arm and the advisory live in the sibling `shared/scripts/classifier-cite-drift-scan.mjs`.\n`validate_document.py` runs the same check as a non-blocking advisory on the document it validates.\nIt prints `cite-drift advisory:` lines only when a Jev credential is stored, never changes the exit code, and `JEV_FEATURE_CITE_DRIFT=0` (older name: `SKDOC_CITE_DRIFT_CHECK=0`) skips it.\nThe `sk-doc/scripts/` root directory keeps facade symlinks pointing inward to `shared/` and the owning packets so tool paths resolve.\nThere are no hub-root `assets/` or `references/` aggregation directories: consumers reference each packet's own `assets/`/`references/` or the `shared/` backbone directly.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RULES\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n- **ALWAYS** resolve a packet through `mode-registry.json`; never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n- **ALWAYS** keep authoring contracts in the packets; the hub stays routing-only.\n- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one identity.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the root `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` in sync with `leaf-manifest.json` and `leaf-aliases.json` — the leaf sets dual-read to canonical typed pairs at the one contract boundary.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the sk-create-quality-control pipeline as one shared source under `shared/`, consumed by the packets.\n- **ALWAYS** keep changelogs as real files at the hub and in each packet — never symlinked.\n\n### ⛔ NEVER\n- **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a packet or `shared/`.\n- **NEVER** put per-packet authoring logic in the hub.\n- **NEVER** add a surface axis or a `surfaceBundle` outcome — sk-doc is workflow-only.\n\n### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n- A new documentation workflow is needed — extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet; do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n- A packet would become a near-empty shell (generic doctrine, no type-specific behavior) — fold it into `shared/` instead.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n- Registry: `mode-registry.json` (fifteen modes across fourteen packets, `packetKind: workflow`).\n- Hub router: `hub-router.json` (signals + vocabulary classes).\n- Surface router: `ROUTER.md` (authoring intent to packet-local leaf sets).\n- Advisor descriptor: `description.json`; skill-graph identity: `graph-metadata.json`.\n- Packets: `sk-create-skill/`, `sk-create-readme/`, `sk-create-agent/`, `sk-create-command/`, `sk-create-feature-catalog/`, `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/`, `sk-create-benchmark/`, `sk-create-changelog/`, `sk-create-diff/`, `sk-create-repo-rule/`, `sk-create-frontmatter/`, `sk-create-goal/`, `sk-create-with-human-voice/`, `sk-create-quality-control/`.\n- Shared backbone: `shared/scripts/`, `shared/references/`, `shared/assets/`.\n- Parent-skill pattern: `sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/failed-runs.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/source.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/source.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>","title":"skill","metadata":{"metadata":{"name":"sk-doc","directory":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc","truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"<skill_content name=\"sk-doc\">\n# Skill: sk-doc\n\n<!-- Keywords: sk-doc, documentation, markdown, authoring, parent-hub, mode-registry, hub-router, workflowmode, packetkind, sk-create-skill, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command, sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark, sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter, sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, sk-create-quality-control, human-voice-rules, hvr, shared-backbone, create-quality-control-pipeline -->\n\n# Documentation Authoring Hub (sk-doc)\n\nOne advisor identity, fifteen workflow modes across fourteen packets, one shared sk-create-quality-control backbone.\n`sk-doc` is the parent hub for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring.\nIt holds NO per-packet logic: it routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each packet keeps its own contract in its nested folder.\nThe cross-cutting sk-create-quality-control pipeline (validators, global standards, frontmatter/llms/template assets) lives once in `shared/` and is consumed by every packet.\n\n**Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`; `description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value, so every hub-root JSON routing file states the same release; `ROUTER.md` keeps its own document version.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring, and for document-quality work. The hub classifies the request, resolves a `workflowMode`, and loads the matching nested packet.\n\n| Mode | Use it for | Packet | Command |\n|------|------------|--------|---------|\n| **sk-create-skill** | Scaffold an OpenCode skill (and, via `sk-create-skill-parent`, a parent hub with nested mode packets) | `sk-create-skill/` | `/create:skill`, `/create:skill-parent` |\n| **sk-create-readme** | Author a folder README, general or code-folder | `sk-create-readme/` | `/create:readme` |\n| **sk-create-agent** | Scaffold an OpenCode agent (permission/authority frontmatter) | `sk-create-agent/` | `/create:agent` |\n| **sk-create-command** | Scaffold an OpenCode slash command (argument-hint + allowed-tools + router/presentation split) | `sk-create-command/` | `/create:command` |\n| **sk-create-feature-catalog** | Author a feature-catalog inventory package | `sk-create-feature-catalog/` | `/create:feature-catalog` |\n| **sk-create-manual-testing-playbook** | Author a manual-testing-playbook package | `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/` | `/create:manual-testing-playbook` |\n| **sk-create-benchmark** | Author MCP-promotion and behavior packages or inputs | `sk-create-benchmark/` | `/create:benchmark` |\n| **sk-create-changelog** | Author a global component changelog entry (version bump + topology-aware placement). Packet-local changelogs are deprecated | `sk-create-changelog/` | `/create:changelog` |\n| **sk-create-diff** | Produce a local, Git-free before/after review of an edited document (text/Markdown/HTML/DOCX/text-PDF) as a self-contained HTML report | `sk-create-diff/` | `/create:diff` |\n| **sk-create-repo-rule** | Create, revise or retire a repo-local rule under `repo-rules/` and wire it into `REPO RULES.md` (four decision tests refuse most requests) | `sk-create-repo-rule/` | `/create:repo-rule` |\n| **sk-create-with-human-voice** | Apply the Human Voice Rules to prose, or score prose against them, with a scope gate and a re-scan | `sk-create-with-human-voice/` | `/create:with-human-voice` |\n| **sk-create-frontmatter** | Own the YAML frontmatter contract: per-class field rules, the description budget, and the 4-part `version` derivation | `sk-create-frontmatter/` | — (routes via aliases) |\n| **sk-create-goal** | Author or revise a packet `goal.md`: the durable directive, three to seven self-contained completion criteria, and the phase-parent binding table (session-goal setting stays with the goal hooks) | `sk-create-goal/` | `/create:goal` |\n| **sk-create-quality-control** | Validate / score / optimize an EXISTING document (extract → DQI → HVR → validate) | `sk-create-quality-control/` | (routes via aliases) |\n\n### When NOT to Use\n- Code implementation, tests, or debugging — use `sk-code`.\n- Git worktree, branch, commit, PR, or release work — use `sk-git`.\n- Spec-folder workflow / memory / save context — use `system-spec-kit`.\n- A measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source) — use `sk-design-md-generator`.\n- The `shared/` backbone is consumed by the packets, not invoked as a user workflow.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nRouting is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\nStage 1 (hub → mode) selects a mode: `hub-router.json` `vocabularyClasses` is the one authored routing vocabulary.\nThe `mode-registry.json` aliases, each packet's `Keyword triggers:` line and the root `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against it.\n`sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-hub-vocabulary-source.cjs` fails on the `alias`, `stage-two`, `triggers` and `packets` legs; its `stage-two-reverse` leg only reports.\nStage 2 (mode → leaves) lives in the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md), which maps the request's authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources that mode loads.\nThe two layers stay separate: the hub never emits leaf paths, and the surface router never re-decides the mode.\nThe advisor routes every documentation and authoring query to the single identity `sk-doc`.\nResolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-doc --prompt \"<task>\"`.\nAct on the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\nCompiled routing is the default.\nAn unclear documentation intent asks for disambiguation, because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\nRun the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Terms\n\n- **`workflowMode`**: the public packet key a request resolves to, such as `sk-create-skill`.\n- **`packetKind`**: `workflow` for every sk-doc packet, because this hub has no surface axis.\n- **`backendKind`**: `template-scaffold` for the create-* generators and `sk-create-quality-control` for the quality-control mode.\n- **packet**: a nested `sk-create-*` folder that keeps its own authoring contract, references, assets and scripts.\n- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each packet's discriminator, aliases, command and `advisorRouting`.\n- **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's signals, vocabulary classes, default fallback resource and bundle rules.\n- **`vocabularyClasses`**: the one authored routing vocabulary that the registry aliases, the `Keyword triggers:` lines and the `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against.\n- **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, which map an authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources its mode loads.\n- **leaf**: a canonical `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n- **`defer`**: the outcome that asks for disambiguation, which this hub reaches because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\n- **shared backbone**: the `shared/` copy of the sk-create-quality-control pipeline that every packet consumes.\n- **`graph-metadata.json`**: the one advisor identity for the whole skill, which no packet or `shared/` directory carries.\n\n### Layout\n```\nsk-doc/\n  SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-packet logic)\n  ROUTER.md              # stage-two surface router (authoring intent -> leaf sets)\n  mode-registry.json     # the fifteen-mode discriminator + advisorRouting (single source of truth)\n  hub-router.json        # router signals + vocabulary classes\n  description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n  graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n  changelog/  manual-testing-playbook/  benchmark/  feature-catalog/\n  sk-create-skill/  sk-create-readme/  sk-create-agent/  sk-create-command/\n  sk-create-feature-catalog/  sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/\n  sk-create-benchmark/  sk-create-changelog/  sk-create-diff/\n  sk-create-repo-rule/  sk-create-frontmatter/  sk-create-goal/\n  sk-create-with-human-voice/  sk-create-quality-control/    # nested workflow packets\n  scripts/               # facade symlinks -> shared/ + owning packets (tool paths only)\n  shared/                # sk-create-quality-control backbone: validators, global standards, shared assets\n```\n\nEach packet is self-contained (its own `SKILL.md`, `README.md`, `changelog/`, and moved `references/`/`assets/`/`scripts/`) and carries **no** `graph-metadata.json`, so the advisor discovers exactly one `sk-doc` identity.\n\n### Shared backbone\n`shared/` holds the universal sk-create-quality-control pipeline consumed by every packet:\n- Generic validator scripts in `shared/scripts/`.\n- Cross-cutting standards and vocabulary in `shared/references/`.\n- Shared templates in `shared/assets/`.\n\nThe generic validator `shared/scripts/cite-drift-scan.mjs` counts and resolves the `<path>.<ext>:<line>` citations in every tracked skill doc.\nIt makes zero model calls by default.\n`--jev` runs the backend and needs `--out <dir>`, so every call is recorded, and the Jev arm and the advisory live in the sibling `shared/scripts/classifier-cite-drift-scan.mjs`.\n`validate_document.py` runs the same check as a non-blocking advisory on the document it validates.\nIt prints `cite-drift advisory:` lines only when a Jev credential is stored, never changes the exit code, and `JEV_FEATURE_CITE_DRIFT=0` (older name: `SKDOC_CITE_DRIFT_CHECK=0`) skips it.\nThe `sk-doc/scripts/` root directory keeps facade symlinks pointing inward to `shared/` and the owning packets so tool paths resolve.\nThere are no hub-root `assets/` or `references/` aggregation directories: consumers reference each packet's own `assets/`/`references/` or the `shared/` backbone directly.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RULES\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n- **ALWAYS** resolve a packet through `mode-registry.json`; never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n- **ALWAYS** keep authoring contracts in the packets; the hub stays routing-only.\n- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one identity.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the root `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` in sync with `leaf-manifest.json` and `leaf-aliases.json` — the leaf sets dual-read to canonical typed pairs at the one contract boundary.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the sk-create-quality-control pipeline as one shared source under `shared/`, consumed by the packets.\n- **ALWAYS** keep changelogs as real files at the hub and in each packet — never symlinked.\n\n### ⛔ NEVER\n- **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a packet or `shared/`.\n- **NEVER** put per-packet authoring logic in the hub.\n- **NEVER** add a surface axis or a `surfaceBundle` outcome — sk-doc is workflow-only.\n\n### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n- A new documentation workflow is needed — extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet; do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n- A packet would become a near-empty shell (generic doctrine, no type-specific behavior) — fold it into `shared/` instead.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n- Registry: `mode-registry.json` (fifteen modes across fourteen packets, `packetKind: workflow`).\n- Hub router: `hub-router.json` (signals + vocabulary classes).\n- Surface router: `ROUTER.md` (authoring intent to packet-local leaf sets).\n- Advisor descriptor: `description.json`; skill-graph identity: `graph-metadata.json`.\n- Packets: `sk-create-skill/`, `sk-create-readme/`, `sk-create-agent/`, `sk-create-command/`, `sk-create-feature-catalog/`, `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/`, `sk-create-benchmark/`, `sk-create-changelog/`, `sk-create-diff/`, `sk-create-repo-rule/`, `sk-create-frontmatter/`, `sk-create-goal/`, `sk-create-with-human-voice/`, `sk-create-quality-control/`.\n- Shared backbone: `shared/scripts/`, `shared/references/`, `shared/assets/`.\n- Parent-skill pattern: `sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/failed-runs.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/source.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/source.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"id\":\"sk-doc\"}"},"time":{"start":1794528544051,"end":1794528544231}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794528546257,"sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","part":{"id":"prt_1d23c8dd1001rPbhLxhx2oodMX","sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","messageID":"msg_1d23c334b001dEKejz7dlpD6dn","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"a91b8ccd8030531f9f7fc67ae8d01d0697fca043","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":14240,"output":123,"reasoning":1664,"cache":{"read":27392,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794528547863,"sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","part":{"id":"prt_1d23c9417001m3ahcPvthOBA6V","sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","messageID":"msg_1d23c8dd5001YsOhmd13yNWguz","type":"step-start","snapshot":"d99a7a4bb5142d57d61cbefbe67207a792be9bed"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794528551995,"sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","part":{"partID":"prt_1d23ca3590015iCNaPZwRucmfE","sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","messageID":"msg_1d23c8dd5001YsOhmd13yNWguz","type":"tool","id":"call_8bcb062775b04aa8a49f3125","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md, lines 1-220\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication handoff\"\n3: description: \"End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"what do i do now\"\n6:   - \"end of turn handback\"\n7:   - \"next steps for the operator\"\n8:   - \"who does what next\"\n9:   - \"the reply ended with nothing to do\"\n10:   - \"buried the ask\"\n11:   - \"i asked in prose\"\n12:   - \"structured choice instead of prose\"\n13:   - \"offer options not paragraphs\"\n14:   - \"waiting on the user\"\n15:   - \"blocked on a decision\"\n16:   - \"needs your approval\"\n17:   - \"unanswered question at the end\"\n18:   - \"should i continue\"\n19:   - \"which option do you want\"\n20:   - \"handing control back\"\n21:   - \"the operator has to act\"\n22:   - \"nothing is blocked on you\"\n23:   - \"state restatement cadence\"\n24:   - \"where were we\"\n25:   - \"catch me up\"\n26:   - \"closing contract\"\n27:   - \"one concrete next action\"\n28:   - \"what is in flight\"\n29:   - \"still running in the background\"\n30:   - \"did it stall or is it working\"\n31:   - \"will you continue on your own\"\n32:   - \"waiting on me or on a machine\"\n33:   - \"resume condition\"\n34: importance_tier: important\n35: contextType: reference\n36: version: 1.6.0.8\n37: ---\n38: \n39: # Rule: Communication handoff\n40: \n41: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before ending a turn that leaves the operator anything to do.\n42: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n43: \n44: ## Fires when\n45: \n46: - About to end a turn, of any kind, substantive or not.\n47: - About to report that work is done, blocked, partially done, or waiting on something.\n48: - About to end a turn while something you started is still running.\n49: - About to ask the operator anything.\n50: - About to state a fork, a trade-off, or two acceptable paths.\n51: - About to continue past a decision that was never actually made, which is when this rule earns its load.\n52: \n53: ## The rule\n54: \n55: **End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.**\n56: \n57: Naming is the obligation. An action the operator has to infer from a status report is an\n58: action that does not happen.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 1. THE HANDBACK IS A SEPARATE THING FROM THE STATUS\n63: \n64: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status, a read-only turn included. `AGENTS.md` §10\n65: requires it and this section lists what it holds. Say each part briefly:\n66: \n67: 1. **What ran or was read, and what it returned**, with the receipts from\n68:    [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §1.\n69: 2. **What is inferred** rather than observed.\n70: 3. **What only the operator can verify.**\n71: 4. **The state of the work:** edited / committed / pushed / dirty, and which branch.\n72: 5. **Known residual risk.** Any risk the operator must weigh before relying on the work, such as an untested path or a state that could not be checked. Write \"none known\" when there is none.\n73: \n74: What was not done belongs in the status as well, and [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §10\n75: says how to report it. The status reports what happened. The handback reports what happens next,\n76: and it is a different document.\n77: \n78: **Restate the state when the reader must re-orient.** It fires when the direction of the\n79: work changes, when two attempts at the same fix have failed or when the work resumes after\n80: a gap. The restatement says what is done, what is open and what changed, and carries the\n81: operator's standing constraints and decisions in their own words. It replaces the previous\n82: stated state. It does not summarize the reply.\n83: \n84: The failure this prevents: a reader who returns mid task and trusts a stated state that\n85: the turns since have superseded.\n86: \n87: **Position it last**, so a reader who stops halfway through a reply has still hit it.\n88: \n89: The close of the turn is a contract with two clauses. Completed work is shown, as the\n90: changed file, the passing check or the output, after the outcome that\n91: [`communication.md`](communication.md) §4 puts first. When a command ran, name the command\n92: and its exit status or result beside the claim it supports, before your reading of it. What happens next is exactly one concrete action, in the form that lets the operator do\n93: it, or the one line that says nothing is.\n94: \n95: The failure this prevents: a close out the operator reads, agrees with and acts on\n96: nothing from, because the work was claimed instead of shown and the next steps\n97: scattered, no single concrete action among them.\n98: \n99: ---\n100: \n101: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS AN OPERATOR ACTION\n102: \n103: Only things the operator does, and only things that are actually theirs.\n104: \n105: | Is an operator action | Is not |\n106: |---|---|\n107: | A decision only they can make | A decision you already made and are narrating |\n108: | A credential, an approval, an access grant | A step you could take and chose not to |\n109: | Running something you cannot run | Work you left undone and are reframing as theirs |\n110: | Reviewing a change before it ships | A summary of what you just did |\n111: \n112: Padding the list with your own remaining work is the common failure: it trains the operator\n113: to skim the one list they need to read.\n114: \n115: ---\n116: \n117: ## 3. NOTHING TO DO IS ALSO AN ANSWER\n118: \n119: When nothing is blocked on the operator, say that in one line and stop.\n120: \n121: The failure this prevents: an operator who reads every reply twice looking for the thing they\n122: were supposed to notice.\n123: \n124: ---\n125: \n126: ## 4. WHEN THE THING YOU NEED IS A DECISION\n127: \n128: **Ask as a structured choice, through the question tool §5 names for your runtime, when all\n129: three hold:**\n130: \n131: - The alternatives are nameable, not open-ended.\n132: - The answer changes what you do next. A question that changes nothing is a delay.\n133: - You cannot resolve it from the request, the code or a sensible default.\n134: \n135: **Otherwise put it in prose and keep going.** `AGENTS.md` §3 already refuses \"should I\n136: continue?\" for a step that is clear and in scope.\n137: \n138: Recommend one option and say why.\n139: \n140: A lettered or numbered menu typed into the reply is not a structured choice when §5 names a\n141: tool for your runtime. It is the buried question in a different shape: the operator has to\n142: type the answer back, and nothing records it as a decision.\n143: \n144: The failure this prevents: two of them. A question nobody answers because it was a sentence in\n145: a paragraph, and a menu that appears for something the operator expected you to handle.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 5. THE QUESTION SURFACE IS PER RUNTIME\n150: \n151: The posture above binds on every runtime. Resolve the surface at the runtime you are in\n152: rather than assuming one.\n153: \n154: | Runtime | Surface | How this is known |\n155: |---|---|---|\n156: | Claude Code | `AskUserQuestion`, in the main conversation only. A subagent has none | Named in this repository |\n157: | Pi | `ask_user_question` from the `@juicesharp/rpiv-ask-user-question` extension, or `question` from `pi-plan-build` | Recorded as installed in `.pi/PLUGINS.md` |\n158: | OpenCode | `question`, built in | The tool name the decision-capture sweep reads, `sweep-opencode.mjs` |\n159: | Codex | `request_user_input`, only on a turn whose tool list names it. Plan mode has it, Default mode only behind the `default_mode_request_user_input` feature, which ships off and this repository's `.codex/config.toml` turns on, and `codex exec` never | Read from the Codex 0.160.1 binary's own instructions |\n160: | Devin | `ask_user_question`. A subagent has none | `cli-devin/references/devin-tools.md` |\n161: | DSH | `ask_user_question`, in a session with an attached client. A headless run has none | `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md` |\n162: | Cursor | None | No question tool in the Cursor tool reference |\n163: | Anything else | No native surface assumed | Use the fallback below |\n164: \n165: **A session with no such surface is not exempt.** That is a runtime the table gives no tool,\n166: or a session where the tool is missing: a subagent, a headless run, `codex exec`. It falls\n167: back to a numbered list of named options with the recommendation marked, and says in one\n168: line that no question tool was available. Where the table names a tool and the session has\n169: it, the typed list is the failure §4 names, never a fallback.\n170: \n171: Do not invent a tool name to fill a gap in that table. An invented name fails silently, the\n172: question is never asked, and the turn ends looking complete. Where the row says unverified,\n173: check first or use the fallback.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 6. WHAT IS STILL RUNNING, AND WHAT RESUMES YOU\n178: \n179: **Name what is in flight.** Anything you started that is still running and not yet\n180: reportable: a background agent, a dispatched lane, a watcher, a long suite, a build. One\n181: item is a line. More than one is a small table, the item and the state it is actually in.\n182: That table is this rule's one carve-out from [`communication.md`](communication.md) §2.\n183: The state is the payload: what it is doing now, how far in it is, what it is waiting on.\n184: \n185: **Say what resumes you.** One line, of the shape \"I will continue autonomously when …\",\n186: naming the event that unblocks you: a lane returning, a suite going green, a watcher firing.\n187: \n188: **Neither is ceremony.** A turn with nothing running writes no table and claims no resume\n189: condition. A turn that genuinely needs a decision says so and stops, and never claims it\n190: will continue by itself.\n191: \n192: The failure this prevents: a turn that goes quiet while work is still running, leaving the\n193: operator unable to tell whether anything is happening or whether it is their move.\n194: \n195: ---\n196: \n197: ## 7. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n198: \n199: - **Not licence to ask more.** The bar in §4 is narrow on purpose, and `AGENTS.md` §2\n200:   consolidates whatever survives it into a single prompt.\n201: - **Not licence to stop early.** Naming what is left is never a substitute for finishing what\n202:   is yours, and `AGENTS.md` §3 refuses partial work framed as a checkpoint.\n203: - **Not a template.** No heading is required, no fixed wording. A reader who can act is the\n204:   only test.\n205: \n206: ---\n207: \n208: ## 8. SELF-CHECK\n209: \n210: - [ ] The status names what ran, what is inferred, what only the operator can verify, the state of the work and any known residual risk. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n211: - [ ] The turn ends by naming what is the operator's to do, or by saying nothing is.\n212: - [ ] Every item on that list is theirs, not mine reframed.\n213: - [ ] The handback is last, and survives a reader who stops halfway.\n214: - [ ] Anything asked as a structured choice passes all three conditions in §4.\n215: - [ ] Any choice I offered carries a recommendation and a reason.\n216: - [ ] Nothing was handed back that I could have decided or done myself.\n217: - [ ] No question-tool name appears that I did not confirm for the runtime I am in.\n218: - [ ] Where the direction changed, two fixes failed or the work resumed after a gap, I restated what is done, what is open and what changed.\n219: - [ ] Anything I started that is still running is named with the state it is in, and nothing appears there that has already finished.\n220: - [ ] A claim that I will continue on my own names the event that resumes me, and no such claim stands on a turn that needs an operator decision.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md, lines 1-220\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication handoff\"\n3: description: \"End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"what do i do now\"\n6:   - \"end of turn handback\"\n7:   - \"next steps for the operator\"\n8:   - \"who does what next\"\n9:   - \"the reply ended with nothing to do\"\n10:   - \"buried the ask\"\n11:   - \"i asked in prose\"\n12:   - \"structured choice instead of prose\"\n13:   - \"offer options not paragraphs\"\n14:   - \"waiting on the user\"\n15:   - \"blocked on a decision\"\n16:   - \"needs your approval\"\n17:   - \"unanswered question at the end\"\n18:   - \"should i continue\"\n19:   - \"which option do you want\"\n20:   - \"handing control back\"\n21:   - \"the operator has to act\"\n22:   - \"nothing is blocked on you\"\n23:   - \"state restatement cadence\"\n24:   - \"where were we\"\n25:   - \"catch me up\"\n26:   - \"closing contract\"\n27:   - \"one concrete next action\"\n28:   - \"what is in flight\"\n29:   - \"still running in the background\"\n30:   - \"did it stall or is it working\"\n31:   - \"will you continue on your own\"\n32:   - \"waiting on me or on a machine\"\n33:   - \"resume condition\"\n34: importance_tier: important\n35: contextType: reference\n36: version: 1.6.0.8\n37: ---\n38: \n39: # Rule: Communication handoff\n40: \n41: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before ending a turn that leaves the operator anything to do.\n42: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n43: \n44: ## Fires when\n45: \n46: - About to end a turn, of any kind, substantive or not.\n47: - About to report that work is done, blocked, partially done, or waiting on something.\n48: - About to end a turn while something you started is still running.\n49: - About to ask the operator anything.\n50: - About to state a fork, a trade-off, or two acceptable paths.\n51: - About to continue past a decision that was never actually made, which is when this rule earns its load.\n52: \n53: ## The rule\n54: \n55: **End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.**\n56: \n57: Naming is the obligation. An action the operator has to infer from a status report is an\n58: action that does not happen.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 1. THE HANDBACK IS A SEPARATE THING FROM THE STATUS\n63: \n64: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status, a read-only turn included. `AGENTS.md` §10\n65: requires it and this section lists what it holds. Say each part briefly:\n66: \n67: 1. **What ran or was read, and what it returned**, with the receipts from\n68:    [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §1.\n69: 2. **What is inferred** rather than observed.\n70: 3. **What only the operator can verify.**\n71: 4. **The state of the work:** edited / committed / pushed / dirty, and which branch.\n72: 5. **Known residual risk.** Any risk the operator must weigh before relying on the work, such as an untested path or a state that could not be checked. Write \"none known\" when there is none.\n73: \n74: What was not done belongs in the status as well, and [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §10\n75: says how to report it. The status reports what happened. The handback reports what happens next,\n76: and it is a different document.\n77: \n78: **Restate the state when the reader must re-orient.** It fires when the direction of the\n79: work changes, when two attempts at the same fix have failed or when the work resumes after\n80: a gap. The restatement says what is done, what is open and what changed, and carries the\n81: operator's standing constraints and decisions in their own words. It replaces the previous\n82: stated state. It does not summarize the reply.\n83: \n84: The failure this prevents: a reader who returns mid task and trusts a stated state that\n85: the turns since have superseded.\n86: \n87: **Position it last**, so a reader who stops halfway through a reply has still hit it.\n88: \n89: The close of the turn is a contract with two clauses. Completed work is shown, as the\n90: changed file, the passing check or the output, after the outcome that\n91: [`communication.md`](communication.md) §4 puts first. When a command ran, name the command\n92: and its exit status or result beside the claim it supports, before your reading of it. What happens next is exactly one concrete action, in the form that lets the operator do\n93: it, or the one line that says nothing is.\n94: \n95: The failure this prevents: a close out the operator reads, agrees with and acts on\n96: nothing from, because the work was claimed instead of shown and the next steps\n97: scattered, no single concrete action among them.\n98: \n99: ---\n100: \n101: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS AN OPERATOR ACTION\n102: \n103: Only things the operator does, and only things that are actually theirs.\n104: \n105: | Is an operator action | Is not |\n106: |---|---|\n107: | A decision only they can make | A decision you already made and are narrating |\n108: | A credential, an approval, an access grant | A step you could take and chose not to |\n109: | Running something you cannot run | Work you left undone and are reframing as theirs |\n110: | Reviewing a change before it ships | A summary of what you just did |\n111: \n112: Padding the list with your own remaining work is the common failure: it trains the operator\n113: to skim the one list they need to read.\n114: \n115: ---\n116: \n117: ## 3. NOTHING TO DO IS ALSO AN ANSWER\n118: \n119: When nothing is blocked on the operator, say that in one line and stop.\n120: \n121: The failure this prevents: an operator who reads every reply twice looking for the thing they\n122: were supposed to notice.\n123: \n124: ---\n125: \n126: ## 4. WHEN THE THING YOU NEED IS A DECISION\n127: \n128: **Ask as a structured choice, through the question tool §5 names for your runtime, when all\n129: three hold:**\n130: \n131: - The alternatives are nameable, not open-ended.\n132: - The answer changes what you do next. A question that changes nothing is a delay.\n133: - You cannot resolve it from the request, the code or a sensible default.\n134: \n135: **Otherwise put it in prose and keep going.** `AGENTS.md` §3 already refuses \"should I\n136: continue?\" for a step that is clear and in scope.\n137: \n138: Recommend one option and say why.\n139: \n140: A lettered or numbered menu typed into the reply is not a structured choice when §5 names a\n141: tool for your runtime. It is the buried question in a different shape: the operator has to\n142: type the answer back, and nothing records it as a decision.\n143: \n144: The failure this prevents: two of them. A question nobody answers because it was a sentence in\n145: a paragraph, and a menu that appears for something the operator expected you to handle.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 5. THE QUESTION SURFACE IS PER RUNTIME\n150: \n151: The posture above binds on every runtime. Resolve the surface at the runtime you are in\n152: rather than assuming one.\n153: \n154: | Runtime | Surface | How this is known |\n155: |---|---|---|\n156: | Claude Code | `AskUserQuestion`, in the main conversation only. A subagent has none | Named in this repository |\n157: | Pi | `ask_user_question` from the `@juicesharp/rpiv-ask-user-question` extension, or `question` from `pi-plan-build` | Recorded as installed in `.pi/PLUGINS.md` |\n158: | OpenCode | `question`, built in | The tool name the decision-capture sweep reads, `sweep-opencode.mjs` |\n159: | Codex | `request_user_input`, only on a turn whose tool list names it. Plan mode has it, Default mode only behind the `default_mode_request_user_input` feature, which ships off and this repository's `.codex/config.toml` turns on, and `codex exec` never | Read from the Codex 0.160.1 binary's own instructions |\n160: | Devin | `ask_user_question`. A subagent has none | `cli-devin/references/devin-tools.md` |\n161: | DSH | `ask_user_question`, in a session with an attached client. A headless run has none | `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md` |\n162: | Cursor | None | No question tool in the Cursor tool reference |\n163: | Anything else | No native surface assumed | Use the fallback below |\n164: \n165: **A session with no such surface is not exempt.** That is a runtime the table gives no tool,\n166: or a session where the tool is missing: a subagent, a headless run, `codex exec`. It falls\n167: back to a numbered list of named options with the recommendation marked, and says in one\n168: line that no question tool was available. Where the table names a tool and the session has\n169: it, the typed list is the failure §4 names, never a fallback.\n170: \n171: Do not invent a tool name to fill a gap in that table. An invented name fails silently, the\n172: question is never asked, and the turn ends looking complete. Where the row says unverified,\n173: check first or use the fallback.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 6. WHAT IS STILL RUNNING, AND WHAT RESUMES YOU\n178: \n179: **Name what is in flight.** Anything you started that is still running and not yet\n180: reportable: a background agent, a dispatched lane, a watcher, a long suite, a build. One\n181: item is a line. More than one is a small table, the item and the state it is actually in.\n182: That table is this rule's one carve-out from [`communication.md`](communication.md) §2.\n183: The state is the payload: what it is doing now, how far in it is, what it is waiting on.\n184: \n185: **Say what resumes you.** One line, of the shape \"I will continue autonomously when …\",\n186: naming the event that unblocks you: a lane returning, a suite going green, a watcher firing.\n187: \n188: **Neither is ceremony.** A turn with nothing running writes no table and claims no resume\n189: condition. A turn that genuinely needs a decision says so and stops, and never claims it\n190: will continue by itself.\n191: \n192: The failure this prevents: a turn that goes quiet while work is still running, leaving the\n193: operator unable to tell whether anything is happening or whether it is their move.\n194: \n195: ---\n196: \n197: ## 7. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n198: \n199: - **Not licence to ask more.** The bar in §4 is narrow on purpose, and `AGENTS.md` §2\n200:   consolidates whatever survives it into a single prompt.\n201: - **Not licence to stop early.** Naming what is left is never a substitute for finishing what\n202:   is yours, and `AGENTS.md` §3 refuses partial work framed as a checkpoint.\n203: - **Not a template.** No heading is required, no fixed wording. A reader who can act is the\n204:   only test.\n205: \n206: ---\n207: \n208: ## 8. SELF-CHECK\n209: \n210: - [ ] The status names what ran, what is inferred, what only the operator can verify, the state of the work and any known residual risk. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n211: - [ ] The turn ends by naming what is the operator's to do, or by saying nothing is.\n212: - [ ] Every item on that list is theirs, not mine reframed.\n213: - [ ] The handback is last, and survives a reader who stops halfway.\n214: - [ ] Anything asked as a structured choice passes all three conditions in §4.\n215: - [ ] Any choice I offered carries a recommendation and a reason.\n216: - [ ] Nothing was handed back that I could have decided or done myself.\n217: - [ ] No question-tool name appears that I did not confirm for the runtime I am in.\n218: - [ ] Where the direction changed, two fixes failed or the work resumed after a gap, I restated what is done, what is open and what changed.\n219: - [ ] Anything I started that is still running is named with the state it is in, and nothing appears there that has already finished.\n220: - [ ] A claim that I will continue on my own names the event that resumes me, and no such claim stands on a turn that needs an operator decision."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794528551769,"end":1794528551995}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794528552020,"sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","part":{"partID":"prt_1d23ca17d001byK6doQn01NlpF","sessionID":"ses_e2dc43bf5ffeX6kMjGW052ltCf","messageID":"msg_1d23c8dd5001YsOhmd13yNWguz","type":"tool","id":"call_694133e33f7c466481ba80f6","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md, lines 1-245\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication\"\n3: description: \"Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"cut filler\"\n6:   - \"empty opener\"\n7:   - \"match length to the question\"\n8:   - \"no tables\"\n9:   - \"can you say that more plainly\"\n10:   - \"I don't follow\"\n11:   - \"too abstract\"\n12:   - \"number the steps\"\n13:   - \"item cap\"\n14:   - \"reply too long\"\n15:   - \"say each fact once\"\n16:   - \"one idea per sentence\"\n17:   - \"plain words\"\n18:   - \"em dash\"\n19:   - \"reads like terminal output\"\n20:   - \"concise is not compressed\"\n21:   - \"recommend one option\"\n22:   - \"buried the verdict\"\n23:   - \"say where you are going\"\n24:   - \"time estimate\"\n25: importance_tier: important\n26: contextType: reference\n27: version: 1.5.0.0\n28: ---\n29: \n30: # Rule: Communication\n31: \n32: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before writing any substantive reply.\n33: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n34: \n35: ## Fires when\n36: \n37: - About to write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out, a status.\n38: - Drafting or editing a sentence a reader will read.\n39: - About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n40: - About to start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside.\n41: - About to report what a long autonomous run found.\n42: - The reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Write so the reader can act after one pass: the answer first, and nothing in the reply\n47: that does not carry information.**\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 1. THE REGISTER YOU ARE IN\n52: \n53: **While working: clipped.** Act rather than narrate. Open with the result, not with\n54: \"I'll now\" or \"Let me\". Batch the tool calls and report at checkpoints.\n55: \n56: **At a boundary: dense.** You are at a boundary whenever the reader is about to decide\n57: something, act on something or take the work over: a handoff, a close-out, a decision\n58: point. Verdict first, then the receipts. Reason about the problem, not about yourself.\n59: Everything else is working.\n60: \n61: **Complex topic, simple words.** When the subject is technical or layered, explain it plainly the first time, not only after the reader asks: what it is, why it matters to them, what they do next. Use a term only when the reader needs it, and define it on first use. Simpler words, same claim: never drop a caveat or a number to get there.\n62: \n63: The failure this prevents: the wrong register. Narration nobody reads, the verdict buried\n64: in working notes or the working notes inflated into a report.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 2. LENGTH\n69: \n70: **Match length to the question.** Length is earned by the reader's need, never by the work you did to get there.\n71: \n72: **Choose by reader impact before you write.** Decide who will read this and what they already hold. Keep what the reader would notice, act on or\n73: decide differently because of. Merge the items that have the same effect for them. How the\n74: work was done stays out unless it changes how far the reader can trust the result.\n75: \n76: **Concise is not compressed.** Concise cuts what the reader does not need and stops. Compressed cuts further and takes the connective tissue with it: the because, the second half of a contrast, the noun that a later \"this\" points back to. After a cut, check the joints. Every cut this file asks for answers to that floor.\n77: \n78: The failure this prevents: the answer is in there, and they did not find it, under a\n79: record of the work they never needed. Or the cut went too far, and the reader rebuilds the\n80: argument the reply no longer states.\n81: \n82: **No tables in a reply,** except the in-flight block in\n83: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §6. Use a sentence for one or two\n84: facts and bullets for parallel items.\n85: \n86: The failure this prevents: the reader parses a grid to learn what one sentence would\n87: have said.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 3. CUT FILLER\n92: \n93: Every sentence carries information. The recurring offenders:\n94: \n95: - **Empty openers:** \"Great question\", \"Let me take a look\", \"I'll now\".\n96: - **Restated summaries:** repeating back what you just said, one abstraction level up.\n97: - **Vague warnings:** \"be careful with this\", \"this can be tricky\", naming no failure. A caveat earns its place by naming a failure that can happen here.\n98: - **Corporate and marketing register:** \"robust\", \"seamless\", \"leverage\", \"best-in-class\".\n99: - **Narrating the obvious:** announcing a tool call the reader can see the result of.\n100: - **Leaked scaffolding:** a runtime line that tells you to plan privately, list what you\n101:   need next or batch your calls is answered in reasoning, never in the reply.\n102: \n103: **Say each fact once.** Each part of the reply has one job: the first lines carry the\n104: outcome, the body carries the detail, and the end carries the receipts and the handback. A\n105: sentence that repeats an earlier one goes, or it says something the reader does not know\n106: yet.\n107: \n108: The failure this prevents: filler trains the reader to skim, and then they skim the\n109: sentence that mattered.\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 4. THE ANSWER FIRST\n114: \n115: **The first line carries the payload.** The answer, the verdict or the action, in the\n116: first sentence, not a label for it. Test: read the first line on its own. If it told you the outcome, it did its job. Four habits break it: announcing (\"I will now\n117: check the tests\"), labelling (\"Overview:\"), the fragment opener (\"Context.\") and the set-up\n118: that promises the good part in a moment.\n119: \n120: **The rest of the outcome follows at once.** What the work concluded, what changed and what\n121: to do next are in the reader's hands by the second line. The receipts come after that, and\n122: the handback comes last, per [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n123: End when the answer is done. The closing-deletion test: a last line that only asks whether\n124: anything else is needed, or recaps what the reply just said, deletes.\n125: \n126: **Reach the verdict by analysis, then put it first.** The order of the reply is not the order of the thinking. If you cannot state the verdict yet, say that. A named uncertainty is a verdict about the state of the evidence.\n127: \n128: **Recommend one path.** Name its main trade-off, and mention an alternative only when it could change the decision. Mark must-do work apart from nice-to-have. State an assumption you made where evidence was missing, because the reader can correct a visible assumption and cannot correct a silent one.\n129: \n130: **An ambiguous request still gets the answer first.** When two readings lead to different answers, answer the likelier one and say in one line which reading you took. When they lead to materially different work, ask before starting it, per [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md) §1. When you deliver a different version of the ask, the first lines say what changed and why.\n131: \n132: The failure this prevents: a payload that waits behind an announcement or a label, a survey\n133: of options where one recommendation was owed, a swapped or misread request nobody catches,\n134: or a last line that is a farewell and adds nothing.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. HOW THE REPLY MOVES\n139: \n140: **Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward.** A reader who lands mid-reply can act on the paragraph in front of them, and each one picks up where the last one landed: what changed, what it implies, what comes next.\n141: \n142: **Number the steps when there is more than one.** Anything the reader must run, check or\n143: answer gets its own number, one step per line, the number at the start of the line. Numbers\n144: bracketed inside a sentence, (1) then (2) then (3), are a paragraph wearing numbers.\n145: \n146: **No group shows more than five items.** Split a longer set into labelled groups of five or\n147: fewer, or show the five that matter most and say in one line how many are held back, \"the\n148: first five of nine\". The rest is retained, not discarded, and appears when the reader asks.\n149: \n150: **Suppress the tangent.** The reply answers the question it was asked. A second issue worth\n151: raising gets offered once, at the end, in one line. The reader decides whether it happens.\n152: \n153: The failure this prevents: the reader assembles the order themselves, loses track of which\n154: step they have done, mistakes a shortened list for the complete one, or waits for the\n155: answer behind a second issue.\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## 6. SENTENCES AND WORDS\n160: \n161: **One idea per sentence.** Short and declarative. When a sentence stacks clauses, split it. Vary the rhythm, and prefer prose when a list would fragment a single argument.\n162: \n163: **State the relation between sentences.** A sentence says how it attaches to the one before: because, but, therefore, for example. When it explains how something works, it names the moving parts: what acts, what it acts on, what changes.\n164: \n165: **Plain words by default.** Reserve exact names for the things that have them: languages, frameworks, APIs, dependencies, commands. Introduce unavoidable jargon one term at a time, in a sentence that defines it by use.\n166: \n167: **No terse machine register.** Replace arrows and symbols standing in for verbs with action words. For stacked noun phrases, say who does what, using the [HVR nominalization guidance](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md#nominalization-and-stacked-compression). Explain each ID, abbreviation or status code in plain words, and say what a bare count or label measures. Turn fragments into complete sentences.\n168: \n169: **Name an identifier only where the reader acts on it.** A path, hash, flag or ID earns its place when the reader will run, open or check it. Elsewhere, say what the thing is. Keep to one or two numbers a sentence, and when the numbers are the point, give each its own sentence or bullet.\n170: \n171: The failure this prevents: a technically correct reply the reader has to decode, where\n172: they supply a link the writer never stated and cannot find the few names and figures they\n173: need.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 7. PUNCTUATION THE READER TRIPS ON\n178: \n179: - **Never use an em dash.** Use a comma, a full stop or a colon, whichever the sentence wanted.\n180: - **No semicolon.** Two sentences, or a conjunction.\n181: - **No serial comma.** Drop it before the `and` or `or` that closes a list.\n182: \n183: The failure this prevents: dashes read as authored voice to a human and as a tell to a\n184: reader who has seen a lot of generated text.\n185: \n186: The full standard is the Human Voice Rules, which `sk-doc` routes to. Load all of it when writing a document. A reply takes its voice half, the directives, vocabulary lists and tell lists, and leaves its document-structure half.\n187: \n188: ---\n189: \n190: ## 8. LONG STRETCHES AND WHAT THEY FOUND\n191: \n192: **Say where you are going before a long stretch.** Before work the reader cannot see inside, post the intended path: a short numbered list of what you will do and what to expect at each checkpoint. Update it when it changes. Clipped means not narrating each step. It never means starting without saying where you are going.\n193: \n194: **Attach a concrete time estimate when the stretch is long.** Minutes or hours, based on a named earlier run, or marked as an assumption. An estimate informs and never gates. Outside that case, do not open a task with one nobody asked for, and never use one to argue against starting. When the task is too big for one pass, name the smallest slice worth delivering, start there and say what is deferred.\n195: \n196: **A synthesis is not a file path.** When a long run produces findings, the message carries the answer, the few findings behind it, the uncertainty that matters and what was ruled out. The artifact path follows them. Per-iteration narration and internal metrics stay out. Where a run has its own completion contract, as the deep-loop modes do, that contract wins.\n197: \n198: The failure this prevents: twenty minutes of silence, then a result the reader has to\n199: reverse-engineer a plan from, or a path and a count handed over in place of a report.\n200: \n201: ---\n202: \n203: ## 9. WHEN THE READER DID NOT FOLLOW\n204: \n205: \"I don't follow\", \"what?\" or \"too abstract\" calls for a change of modality: a concrete\n206: example, a numbered sequence, a smaller first step or a picture using the runtime's visual\n207: capability. Do not repeat the same explanation at greater length.\n208: \n209: \"say that more plainly\", \"in simple terms\" or \"rewrite that\" calls for a plain\n210: re-render: a copy edit that rewords without reordering, cutting or adding. Every claim,\n211: number, caveat and conclusion keeps its strength. Code, commands, paths, identifiers,\n212: quotations and numbers stay byte-exact. The wording standard is\n213: [hvr-rules.md](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md). If a\n214: plainer word would change what a sentence claims, keep the original.\n215: \n216: The failure this prevents: the same explanation repeats, or the plain rewrite changes the claim.\n217: \n218: ---\n219: \n220: ## 10. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n221: \n222: - **Not a constraint on rigor.** Nothing here softens a claim, a caveat, or a verification standard owned by [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) or [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n223: - **Not a voice to perform.** When honoring a rule here would weaken the answer, keep the answer.\n224: - **Not a license to omit.** \"Match length to the question\" is about the reader's need,\n225:   never about leaving out what they have to know.\n226: - **Not licence to skip the analysis.** Verdict first is an ordering of the writing, never\n227:   a shortcut in the reasoning.\n228: \n229: ---\n230: \n231: ## 11. SELF-CHECK\n232: \n233: - [ ] The first line carries the answer or the action, not a label, an announcement or a setup, and the outcome is complete by the second line.\n234: - [ ] I reached the verdict by analysis, recommended one path with its trade-off and stated the assumptions I made.\n235: - [ ] Where the request was ambiguous or I delivered a different version of it, the first lines say which reading I took and why.\n236: - [ ] Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward, and multi-step work reads as a numbered list.\n237: - [ ] No group runs past five items, nothing was dropped to keep it under, and a tangent appears once at the end or not at all.\n238: - [ ] Every sentence carries information, each fact appears once, and no runtime instruction to plan privately was copied into the reply.\n239: - [ ] Length matches what the reader asked, no table stands in a reply, and nothing was compressed past the point where the reader has to re-expand it.\n240: - [ ] Every sentence carries one idea in plain words and says how it attaches to the one before.\n241: - [ ] Arrows, bare IDs, counts and fragments were turned into sentences, and every path, hash or flag is one the reader will act on.\n242: - [ ] No em dash, no semicolon, no serial comma.\n243: - [ ] Before a long stretch I said where I was going, with a concrete time estimate when it was long, and no estimate argued against starting.\n244: - [ ] A synthesis I reported carries its findings, not just its path and its counts.\n245: - [ ] When the reader did not follow, I changed modality, and a plain re-render kept every claim, number and caveat at the same strength.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md, lines 1-245\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication\"\n3: description: \"Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"cut filler\"\n6:   - \"empty opener\"\n7:   - \"match length to the question\"\n8:   - \"no tables\"\n9:   - \"can you say that more plainly\"\n10:   - \"I don't follow\"\n11:   - \"too abstract\"\n12:   - \"number the steps\"\n13:   - \"item cap\"\n14:   - \"reply too long\"\n15:   - \"say each fact once\"\n16:   - \"one idea per sentence\"\n17:   - \"plain words\"\n18:   - \"em dash\"\n19:   - \"reads like terminal output\"\n20:   - \"concise is not compressed\"\n21:   - \"recommend one option\"\n22:   - \"buried the verdict\"\n23:   - \"say where you are going\"\n24:   - \"time estimate\"\n25: importance_tier: important\n26: contextType: reference\n27: version: 1.5.0.0\n28: ---\n29: \n30: # Rule: Communication\n31: \n32: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before writing any substantive reply.\n33: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n34: \n35: ## Fires when\n36: \n37: - About to write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out, a status.\n38: - Drafting or editing a sentence a reader will read.\n39: - About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n40: - About to start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside.\n41: - About to report what a long autonomous run found.\n42: - The reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Write so the reader can act after one pass: the answer first, and nothing in the reply\n47: that does not carry information.**\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 1. THE REGISTER YOU ARE IN\n52: \n53: **While working: clipped.** Act rather than narrate. Open with the result, not with\n54: \"I'll now\" or \"Let me\". Batch the tool calls and report at checkpoints.\n55: \n56: **At a boundary: dense.** You are at a boundary whenever the reader is about to decide\n57: something, act on something or take the work over: a handoff, a close-out, a decision\n58: point. Verdict first, then the receipts. Reason about the problem, not about yourself.\n59: Everything else is working.\n60: \n61: **Complex topic, simple words.** When the subject is technical or layered, explain it plainly the first time, not only after the reader asks: what it is, why it matters to them, what they do next. Use a term only when the reader needs it, and define it on first use. Simpler words, same claim: never drop a caveat or a number to get there.\n62: \n63: The failure this prevents: the wrong register. Narration nobody reads, the verdict buried\n64: in working notes or the working notes inflated into a report.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 2. LENGTH\n69: \n70: **Match length to the question.** Length is earned by the reader's need, never by the work you did to get there.\n71: \n72: **Choose by reader impact before you write.** Decide who will read this and what they already hold. Keep what the reader would notice, act on or\n73: decide differently because of. Merge the items that have the same effect for them. How the\n74: work was done stays out unless it changes how far the reader can trust the result.\n75: \n76: **Concise is not compressed.** Concise cuts what the reader does not need and stops. Compressed cuts further and takes the connective tissue with it: the because, the second half of a contrast, the noun that a later \"this\" points back to. After a cut, check the joints. Every cut this file asks for answers to that floor.\n77: \n78: The failure this prevents: the answer is in there, and they did not find it, under a\n79: record of the work they never needed. Or the cut went too far, and the reader rebuilds the\n80: argument the reply no longer states.\n81: \n82: **No tables in a reply,** except the in-flight block in\n83: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §6. Use a sentence for one or two\n84: facts and bullets for parallel items.\n85: \n86: The failure this prevents: the reader parses a grid to learn what one sentence would\n87: have said.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 3. CUT FILLER\n92: \n93: Every sentence carries information. The recurring offenders:\n94: \n95: - **Empty openers:** \"Great question\", \"Let me take a look\", \"I'll now\".\n96: - **Restated summaries:** repeating back what you just said, one abstraction level up.\n97: - **Vague warnings:** \"be careful with this\", \"this can be tricky\", naming no failure. A caveat earns its place by naming a failure that can happen here.\n98: - **Corporate and marketing register:** \"robust\", \"seamless\", \"leverage\", \"best-in-class\".\n99: - **Narrating the obvious:** announcing a tool call the reader can see the result of.\n100: - **Leaked scaffolding:** a runtime line that tells you to plan privately, list what you\n101:   need next or batch your calls is answered in reasoning, never in the reply.\n102: \n103: **Say each fact once.** Each part of the reply has one job: the first lines carry the\n104: outcome, the body carries the detail, and the end carries the receipts and the handback. A\n105: sentence that repeats an earlier one goes, or it says something the reader does not know\n106: yet.\n107: \n108: The failure this prevents: filler trains the reader to skim, and then they skim the\n109: sentence that mattered.\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 4. THE ANSWER FIRST\n114: \n115: **The first line carries the payload.** The answer, the verdict or the action, in the\n116: first sentence, not a label for it. Test: read the first line on its own. If it told you the outcome, it did its job. Four habits break it: announcing (\"I will now\n117: check the tests\"), labelling (\"Overview:\"), the fragment opener (\"Context.\") and the set-up\n118: that promises the good part in a moment.\n119: \n120: **The rest of the outcome follows at once.** What the work concluded, what changed and what\n121: to do next are in the reader's hands by the second line. The receipts come after that, and\n122: the handback comes last, per [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n123: End when the answer is done. The closing-deletion test: a last line that only asks whether\n124: anything else is needed, or recaps what the reply just said, deletes.\n125: \n126: **Reach the verdict by analysis, then put it first.** The order of the reply is not the order of the thinking. If you cannot state the verdict yet, say that. A named uncertainty is a verdict about the state of the evidence.\n127: \n128: **Recommend one path.** Name its main trade-off, and mention an alternative only when it could change the decision. Mark must-do work apart from nice-to-have. State an assumption you made where evidence was missing, because the reader can correct a visible assumption and cannot correct a silent one.\n129: \n130: **An ambiguous request still gets the answer first.** When two readings lead to different answers, answer the likelier one and say in one line which reading you took. When they lead to materially different work, ask before starting it, per [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md) §1. When you deliver a different version of the ask, the first lines say what changed and why.\n131: \n132: The failure this prevents: a payload that waits behind an announcement or a label, a survey\n133: of options where one recommendation was owed, a swapped or misread request nobody catches,\n134: or a last line that is a farewell and adds nothing.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. HOW THE REPLY MOVES\n139: \n140: **Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward.** A reader who lands mid-reply can act on the paragraph in front of them, and each one picks up where the last one landed: what changed, what it implies, what comes next.\n141: \n142: **Number the steps when there is more than one.** Anything the reader must run, check or\n143: answer gets its own number, one step per line, the number at the start of the line. Numbers\n144: bracketed inside a sentence, (1) then (2) then (3), are a paragraph wearing numbers.\n145: \n146: **No group shows more than five items.** Split a longer set into labelled groups of five or\n147: fewer, or show the five that matter most and say in one line how many are held back, \"the\n148: first five of nine\". The rest is retained, not discarded, and appears when the reader asks.\n149: \n150: **Suppress the tangent.** The reply answers the question it was asked. A second issue worth\n151: raising gets offered once, at the end, in one line. The reader decides whether it happens.\n152: \n153: The failure this prevents: the reader assembles the order themselves, loses track of which\n154: step they have done, mistakes a shortened list for the complete one, or waits for the\n155: answer behind a second issue.\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## 6. SENTENCE